window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2026-06-1", "source": "2026年06月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence \"With the trend of Urbanization, it is vitally important for city residents to develop a stronger love for Nature.\" You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words. You should copy the sentence given in quotes at the beginning of your essay.", "sample": "With the trend of Urbanization, it is vitally important for city residents to develop a stronger love for\nNature. As concrete forests expand, many urban dwellers rarely feel the breeze, hear birds, or touch soil. A\nstronger bond with nature is not a luxury; it is essential for mental and physical well-being in an increasingly\nartificial environment.\nOne practical way to cultivate this love is to make nature part of daily routines. For example, residents can\nwalk in nearby parks, grow plants on balconies, or join community gardening projects. These activities\nreconnect people with the living world. Last year, I started visiting a small riverbank park every weekend.\nWatching seasons change there reduced my stress and made me more aware of environmental protection.\nTherefore, city governments and individuals should work together to create more green spaces and spend time\nin them. Only by integrating nature into urban life can residents truly develop a stronger love for it and build\nhealthier cities for future generations."}, "translation": {"prompt": "科技进步使中国的医疗事业得到快速发展。近年来，远程医疗使优质医疗资源惠及更多患者。医学专家可以通过视频为偏远地区患者提供咨询，指导当地医生制定并实施治疗方案。人工智能辅助诊断系统帮助医生识别患者的早期症状，提高诊断准确率。电子健康档案的普及让医生能够快速了解患者病史，避免重复检查。线上医疗咨询平台极大地方便了患者就诊。科技与医疗的深度融合更好地满足了人民的健康需求。", "sample": "Advances in science and technology have brought about the rapid development of China's medical services. In recent years, telemedicine has made high-quality medical resources accessible to more patients. Through video links, medical experts can provide consultations for patients in remote areas, guiding local doctors in developing and implementing treatment plans. AI-assisted diagnosis systems help doctors identify patients' early symptoms, thereby improving the accuracy of diagnosis. The widespread adoption of electronic health records enables doctors to quickly review patients' medical histories, thus avoiding repeated examinations. Online medical consultation platforms have also greatly facilitated patients' access to medical care. Overall, the deep integration of technology and healthcare has better met people's health needs."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2026-06-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Two proposals are before the state legislature that look to put some limits on ever-increasing rent prices, which some lawmakers say are contributing to rising homelessness rates.\nThe proposals  __26__  caps on rent increases, preventing landlords in the state from raising rent more than the rate of inflation in most cases. There are exceptions for buildings less than 10 years old, public housing, improvements beyond maintenance, and a hardship  __27__  .\nIn a 2020 study, researchers at the U.S. Government Accountability Office estimated that every $100 in rent increase is  __28__  to a 9% rise in homelessness. Rent increases have an even more detrimental effect on some demographic groups, such as racial minorities.\nRepresentative Alex Ramel explained why he thought that this kind of legislation was so important, telling the story of a  __29__  struggling to live in the community.\nRamel pointed out the  __30__  effect of double-digit rent increases, saying that he made the decision to introduce the legislation while talking to a young man in Bellingham. \"He's a recent graduate from Western Washington University and just got a new job,\" Ramel said. \"He was leaving our community because his landlord  __31__  him that his rent was going to go up by $600 a month.\"\nThe Rental Housing Association of Washington opposes the legislation. The association  __32__  that the policy won't actually solve the problem of rental prices. Instead, it will punish small landlords and allow wealthy renters easy access to cheap housing.\n\"The rent control policy has failed everywhere it has been  __33__  in this county. It has not fixed one housing problem  __34__  . It also does not distinguish between rich and poor when  __35__  who gets the benefits of rent control,\" said Chester Baldwin with the Rental Housing Coalition.", "matchIntro": "Meet the \"digital nomads\" who travel the world in search of fast Wi-Fi", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "For those \"citizens of the world\", the office can be anything from a beach hut in Brisbane to a Starbucks in Seattle, thanks to the growing prevalence of remote-based work. But roaming the globe from café to café is not without its challenges."}, {"label": "B", "text": "When the average traveller lands in a city like Bangkok, Berlin, or Buenos Aires, their first step might be to drop their bags at their hotel and to log on to TripAdvisor to find out the hours of a popular tourist attraction or the best rooftop bar for happy hour."}, {"label": "C", "text": "But people like Pieter Levels are not your average traveller. A Dutch website developer, Levels has been living and working nomadically(像游牧民那样) in places like Singapore, Hong Kong, Tokyo and Manila for two years. With just a carry-on backpack, he chooses his location at will, based on a variety of factors—Wi-Fi speed, average temperature, exotic food and cost of living—that have little bearing on the work he actually does."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Somewhere in his nomadic journey, Levels noticed that there was a growing need to create an online infrastructure for people like himself. That's when he created #nomads, a membership-based chat system which runs on the team communication platform known as Slack. The channel allows users to ask people in their current city the types of practical questions that a tourist would unlikely need to ask."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Members of the geographically dispersed subculture that Levels is a part of call themselves \"digital nomads\". They are borderless, mostly self-employed remote workers who interact with cities in a way that's entirely different from a tourist, but still doesn't quite reach the status of resident. A relatively new phenomenon, there are no hard statistics on the prevalence of digital nomads or location independent workers. But thanks to the growing prevalence of freelance(自由职业的) livelihoods, a global sharing economy and numerous digital tools, the number of people who are able to live this life is growing quickly. And in a rapidly urbanising world, where a major city in Asia increasingly offers the same comforts as one in Europe, many people are reaching the realisation they don't need to stay in one place."}, {"label": "F", "text": "So how does a nomad differ from a tourist or resident? In many ways, Levels says, you can think of a digital nomad like a \"short-term independent expat(移居国外的人)\". Unlike a resident expat, they don't work for a large multinational company in an air conditioned office building and they generally enter a country on a tourist visa, simply renewing or moving on when it expires. But unlike a tourist, they don't arrive in a foreign city without any responsibilities. Travel isn't necessarily required for them to do their jobs, but neither is staying in one place."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Janet Brent is an independent ebook designer. She says many people she knows assume she travels for work, but in reality her international destinations are largely up to her, and driven by cost. \"Contrary to what some people might think, I don't get paid to travel—I simply choose the lifestyle. Rather than rent in one given city, I choose to travel and do short-term, temporary stays around the world, and my travel decisions are literally based on what makes the most sense financially for me,\" Brent said. \"It's completely different from vacationing—you really sink into the city life and get your feet wet in the communities.\""}, {"label": "H", "text": "Bryce Adams is another such nomad. He works as a developer for a South African company WooThemes, which has a workforce that's more than 80% remote-based. Though Adams frequently posts pictures on Instagram of his enviable cooking adventures from Seoul to Bangkok, he says nomad life in foreign cities has its downsides, including finding adequate Wi-Fi for the bandwidth-hungry work his livelihood depends on. Contrary to the perception that he works under a mango tree in a tropical haven, Adams says the reality of this life means that he often ends up staying in more western style places in globalised cities—ones that are sure to have air conditioning and a decent connection—rather than exotic beachfront cafés."}, {"label": "I", "text": "\"A fellow nomad posted quite a funny but honest message on Twitter a few days ago that stuck with me: 'It's funny that digital nomads spend 16 hours to fly to the other side of the world to sit in Starbucks,' and it's completely true,\" Adams said. \"When you're a digital nomad, you don't actually have as much freedom as most people think you do. We have deadlines, we all have people relying on us and a mouth (or two) that needs to be fed. It's nice to be able to work anywhere in the world but it's not without its challenges.\""}, {"label": "J", "text": "But these challenges can often provide an opportunity for nomads to see a side of a city they may not have if they were staying in a luxury hotel for a long weekend. However inconvenient, a trip to a local doctor, immigration office or laundry—or a conflict with a local Airbnb host (the accommodation style of choice for most nomads)—can be far more culturally instructive than a trip to the national museum. And because nomads often have to stay where the strongest internet connection is, they tend to spend more time in developing cities compared to the average tourist, who often flees to more touristy destinations soon after landing at the airport."}, {"label": "K", "text": "The nomad life is, by all accounts, one built out of privilege. You not only need a relatively powerful passport that makes tourist visas easy to attain, but also a knowledge economy job and access to the technology and devices that make remote working possible. But nomads like Adams say that while they are aware of this privilege, they believe living this way elevates what they're able to offer, by giving them a broader perspective on what they're building, writing, or designing and expanding their world view."}, {"label": "L", "text": "\"Being able to travel and get around makes it easy to stay inspired,\" he added. \"I truly believe that it makes me a more sensible human being, as I tend to see and appreciate the creative solutions people come up with in other parts of the world, even though many tend to have far more constraints than we have at home.\""}, {"label": "M", "text": "So when you're constantly on the road and don't own any property or have a fixed address, which city do you call home? It's no surprise that many nomads are dual citizens or \"third culture kids,\" who have grown up with a notion of identity that's not tied to nationalism or staying in one place. Levels says that the nomad life has not only changed his notions of citizenship, but the people he chooses to spend time with as well. \"Saying you're a 'citizen of the world' is a super cliché, right? But it's becoming quite true,\" Levels said. \"This global awareness has been the reason I started Nomad List and it's affected who my friends are. It's normal to have dinner now and sit with a Korean girl, a British-Singaporean guy, a Mexican girl and a Nigerian guy. As much as that sounds like a politically correct TV advertisement, it's great that that's the time we're in now.\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Children's use of social media is a problem. That doesn't mean it should be stopped. While younger children should not be on platforms where 13 is the minimum age, it is neither practical nor desirable to imagine a world in which teenagers are prevented from accessing the platforms and messaging systems that the adults around them use to organise their lives.\nBut the pleasure and stimulation that people of all ages find on their screens and smartphones must not blind us to the harm that spending time in this way can cause. The finding that the alarmingly high rate of depression in British teenage girls is closely correlated to time spent on social media is extremely concerning. Research found that two in five girls are on social media for at least three hours a day—with half as many boys engaged to the same extent. More than a third of depressed girls have experienced online harassment. About half of affected girls suffered disrupted sleep, compared with 20% of depressed boys.\nAlthough the research does not prove that social media causes depression, the importance of the finding that there is a connection should not be underestimated. Schools have done important work in communicating key messages about the risks of online abuse. But when it comes to broader questions about \"screen time\", many professionals as well as parents are confused and uncertain.\nThe health secretary, Matt Hancock, wants official guidelines on time and age limits. This idea has the virtue of simplicity, though some experts question its effects and call for a greater degree of differentiation, arguing that time online by itself is not a bad thing and it all depends on what you are doing. Also due this year is information commissioner Elizabeth Denham's Age Appropriate Design Code, covering the use of children's personal data. Both initiatives are welcome. But the government is playing catch-up. Statutory regulation of the digital media giants is long overdue. The failure to protect children from data harvesting and targeted advertising thus far is as shocking as the lack of concern shown by the companies themselves with regard to children's psychological health.\nOf course, social media companies are not the source of all our ills, and can offer a too-easy target for politicians keen to divert attention from inadequately resourced schools and health services. There are other reasons for the rising incidence of depression in young people, with increasing pressure to succeed in education and in life more broadly—to be famous, beautiful, rich—often cited as a source of unhappiness or anxiety which can, if aggravated by other factors and not managed, tip into ill health. Safeguarding means more than the avoidance of physical threat.", "47": "Children's use of social media is a problem. That doesn't mean it should be stopped. While younger children should not be on platforms where 13 is the minimum age, it is neither practical nor desirable to imagine a world in which teenagers are prevented from accessing the platforms and messaging systems that the adults around them use to organise their lives.\nBut the pleasure and stimulation that people of all ages find on their screens and smartphones must not blind us to the harm that spending time in this way can cause. The finding that the alarmingly high rate of depression in British teenage girls is closely correlated to time spent on social media is extremely concerning. Research found that two in five girls are on social media for at least three hours a day—with half as many boys engaged to the same extent. More than a third of depressed girls have experienced online harassment. About half of affected girls suffered disrupted sleep, compared with 20% of depressed boys.\nAlthough the research does not prove that social media causes depression, the importance of the finding that there is a connection should not be underestimated. Schools have done important work in communicating key messages about the risks of online abuse. But when it comes to broader questions about \"screen time\", many professionals as well as parents are confused and uncertain.\nThe health secretary, Matt Hancock, wants official guidelines on time and age limits. This idea has the virtue of simplicity, though some experts question its effects and call for a greater degree of differentiation, arguing that time online by itself is not a bad thing and it all depends on what you are doing. Also due this year is information commissioner Elizabeth Denham's Age Appropriate Design Code, covering the use of children's personal data. Both initiatives are welcome. But the government is playing catch-up. Statutory regulation of the digital media giants is long overdue. The failure to protect children from data harvesting and targeted advertising thus far is as shocking as the lack of concern shown by the companies themselves with regard to children's psychological health.\nOf course, social media companies are not the source of all our ills, and can offer a too-easy target for politicians keen to divert attention from inadequately resourced schools and health services. There are other reasons for the rising incidence of depression in young people, with increasing pressure to succeed in education and in life more broadly—to be famous, beautiful, rich—often cited as a source of unhappiness or anxiety which can, if aggravated by other factors and not managed, tip into ill health. Safeguarding means more than the avoidance of physical threat.", "48": "Children's use of social media is a problem. That doesn't mean it should be stopped. While younger children should not be on platforms where 13 is the minimum age, it is neither practical nor desirable to imagine a world in which teenagers are prevented from accessing the platforms and messaging systems that the adults around them use to organise their lives.\nBut the pleasure and stimulation that people of all ages find on their screens and smartphones must not blind us to the harm that spending time in this way can cause. The finding that the alarmingly high rate of depression in British teenage girls is closely correlated to time spent on social media is extremely concerning. Research found that two in five girls are on social media for at least three hours a day—with half as many boys engaged to the same extent. More than a third of depressed girls have experienced online harassment. About half of affected girls suffered disrupted sleep, compared with 20% of depressed boys.\nAlthough the research does not prove that social media causes depression, the importance of the finding that there is a connection should not be underestimated. Schools have done important work in communicating key messages about the risks of online abuse. But when it comes to broader questions about \"screen time\", many professionals as well as parents are confused and uncertain.\nThe health secretary, Matt Hancock, wants official guidelines on time and age limits. This idea has the virtue of simplicity, though some experts question its effects and call for a greater degree of differentiation, arguing that time online by itself is not a bad thing and it all depends on what you are doing. Also due this year is information commissioner Elizabeth Denham's Age Appropriate Design Code, covering the use of children's personal data. Both initiatives are welcome. But the government is playing catch-up. Statutory regulation of the digital media giants is long overdue. The failure to protect children from data harvesting and targeted advertising thus far is as shocking as the lack of concern shown by the companies themselves with regard to children's psychological health.\nOf course, social media companies are not the source of all our ills, and can offer a too-easy target for politicians keen to divert attention from inadequately resourced schools and health services. There are other reasons for the rising incidence of depression in young people, with increasing pressure to succeed in education and in life more broadly—to be famous, beautiful, rich—often cited as a source of unhappiness or anxiety which can, if aggravated by other factors and not managed, tip into ill health. Safeguarding means more than the avoidance of physical threat.", "49": "Children's use of social media is a problem. That doesn't mean it should be stopped. While younger children should not be on platforms where 13 is the minimum age, it is neither practical nor desirable to imagine a world in which teenagers are prevented from accessing the platforms and messaging systems that the adults around them use to organise their lives.\nBut the pleasure and stimulation that people of all ages find on their screens and smartphones must not blind us to the harm that spending time in this way can cause. The finding that the alarmingly high rate of depression in British teenage girls is closely correlated to time spent on social media is extremely concerning. Research found that two in five girls are on social media for at least three hours a day—with half as many boys engaged to the same extent. More than a third of depressed girls have experienced online harassment. About half of affected girls suffered disrupted sleep, compared with 20% of depressed boys.\nAlthough the research does not prove that social media causes depression, the importance of the finding that there is a connection should not be underestimated. Schools have done important work in communicating key messages about the risks of online abuse. But when it comes to broader questions about \"screen time\", many professionals as well as parents are confused and uncertain.\nThe health secretary, Matt Hancock, wants official guidelines on time and age limits. This idea has the virtue of simplicity, though some experts question its effects and call for a greater degree of differentiation, arguing that time online by itself is not a bad thing and it all depends on what you are doing. Also due this year is information commissioner Elizabeth Denham's Age Appropriate Design Code, covering the use of children's personal data. Both initiatives are welcome. But the government is playing catch-up. Statutory regulation of the digital media giants is long overdue. The failure to protect children from data harvesting and targeted advertising thus far is as shocking as the lack of concern shown by the companies themselves with regard to children's psychological health.\nOf course, social media companies are not the source of all our ills, and can offer a too-easy target for politicians keen to divert attention from inadequately resourced schools and health services. There are other reasons for the rising incidence of depression in young people, with increasing pressure to succeed in education and in life more broadly—to be famous, beautiful, rich—often cited as a source of unhappiness or anxiety which can, if aggravated by other factors and not managed, tip into ill health. Safeguarding means more than the avoidance of physical threat.", "50": "Children's use of social media is a problem. That doesn't mean it should be stopped. While younger children should not be on platforms where 13 is the minimum age, it is neither practical nor desirable to imagine a world in which teenagers are prevented from accessing the platforms and messaging systems that the adults around them use to organise their lives.\nBut the pleasure and stimulation that people of all ages find on their screens and smartphones must not blind us to the harm that spending time in this way can cause. The finding that the alarmingly high rate of depression in British teenage girls is closely correlated to time spent on social media is extremely concerning. Research found that two in five girls are on social media for at least three hours a day—with half as many boys engaged to the same extent. More than a third of depressed girls have experienced online harassment. About half of affected girls suffered disrupted sleep, compared with 20% of depressed boys.\nAlthough the research does not prove that social media causes depression, the importance of the finding that there is a connection should not be underestimated. Schools have done important work in communicating key messages about the risks of online abuse. But when it comes to broader questions about \"screen time\", many professionals as well as parents are confused and uncertain.\nThe health secretary, Matt Hancock, wants official guidelines on time and age limits. This idea has the virtue of simplicity, though some experts question its effects and call for a greater degree of differentiation, arguing that time online by itself is not a bad thing and it all depends on what you are doing. Also due this year is information commissioner Elizabeth Denham's Age Appropriate Design Code, covering the use of children's personal data. Both initiatives are welcome. But the government is playing catch-up. Statutory regulation of the digital media giants is long overdue. The failure to protect children from data harvesting and targeted advertising thus far is as shocking as the lack of concern shown by the companies themselves with regard to children's psychological health.\nOf course, social media companies are not the source of all our ills, and can offer a too-easy target for politicians keen to divert attention from inadequately resourced schools and health services. There are other reasons for the rising incidence of depression in young people, with increasing pressure to succeed in education and in life more broadly—to be famous, beautiful, rich—often cited as a source of unhappiness or anxiety which can, if aggravated by other factors and not managed, tip into ill health. Safeguarding means more than the avoidance of physical threat.", "51": "About a decade ago, the G-20, a forum of the world's biggest economies, agreed to crack down on multinational corporations' abusive use of tax havens. This resulted in a plan that aimed to curb practices that shielded a large chunk of corporate profits from tax authorities.\nBut, instead of reining in the use of tax havens—countries such as the Bahamas and Cayman Islands with very low or no effective tax rates—the problem has only gotten worse.\nIn a new study, we measured the excessive profits reported in tax havens that cannot be explained by ordinary economic activity such as employees, factories and research in that country. Our findings show a striking pattern of artificial shifting of paper profits to tax havens by corporations, which has been relentless since the 1980s.\nWe found that the world's biggest multinational businesses shifted 37% of the profits—$969 billion—they earned in other countries to tax havens in 2019. The figure was less than 2% in the 1970s. The main reasons for the large increase were the growth of the tax avoidance industry in the 1980s and U.S. policies that made it easier to shift profits from high-tax countries to tax havens.\nWe estimate that the amount of corporate taxes lost as a result reached 10% of total corporate revenue in 2019, up from less than 0.1% in the 1970s. In 2019, the total government tax loss globally was $250 billion. U.S. multinational corporations alone accounted for about half of that, followed by the U.K. and Germany.\nHow do policymakers fix this? So far, the world as a whole has been trying to solve this problem by cutting or scrapping corporate taxes in a very gradual way. In the past 40 years, the global effective corporate tax rate has fallen from 23% to 17%. At the same time, governments have relied more heavily on consumption taxes.\nBut the root cause of profit-shifting is the incentives involved, such as generous or lenient corporate tax rates in other countries. If countries could agree on a global minimum corporate tax rate of, say, 20%, the problem of profit-shifting would largely disappear, as tax havens would simply cease to exist.\nThis type of mechanism is exactly what more than 130 countries signed onto in 2021, with implementation of a 15% minimum tax set to begin soon in the EU, U.K., Japan, Indonesia and many other countries. The U.S. has notably not been able to pass this legislation.\nOur research suggests implementing this type of tax reform is necessary in reversing the shift of ever-greater amounts of corporate profits to tax havens—instead of being taxed by the governments where they operate and create value.", "52": "About a decade ago, the G-20, a forum of the world's biggest economies, agreed to crack down on multinational corporations' abusive use of tax havens. This resulted in a plan that aimed to curb practices that shielded a large chunk of corporate profits from tax authorities.\nBut, instead of reining in the use of tax havens—countries such as the Bahamas and Cayman Islands with very low or no effective tax rates—the problem has only gotten worse.\nIn a new study, we measured the excessive profits reported in tax havens that cannot be explained by ordinary economic activity such as employees, factories and research in that country. Our findings show a striking pattern of artificial shifting of paper profits to tax havens by corporations, which has been relentless since the 1980s.\nWe found that the world's biggest multinational businesses shifted 37% of the profits—$969 billion—they earned in other countries to tax havens in 2019. The figure was less than 2% in the 1970s. The main reasons for the large increase were the growth of the tax avoidance industry in the 1980s and U.S. policies that made it easier to shift profits from high-tax countries to tax havens.\nWe estimate that the amount of corporate taxes lost as a result reached 10% of total corporate revenue in 2019, up from less than 0.1% in the 1970s. In 2019, the total government tax loss globally was $250 billion. U.S. multinational corporations alone accounted for about half of that, followed by the U.K. and Germany.\nHow do policymakers fix this? So far, the world as a whole has been trying to solve this problem by cutting or scrapping corporate taxes in a very gradual way. In the past 40 years, the global effective corporate tax rate has fallen from 23% to 17%. At the same time, governments have relied more heavily on consumption taxes.\nBut the root cause of profit-shifting is the incentives involved, such as generous or lenient corporate tax rates in other countries. If countries could agree on a global minimum corporate tax rate of, say, 20%, the problem of profit-shifting would largely disappear, as tax havens would simply cease to exist.\nThis type of mechanism is exactly what more than 130 countries signed onto in 2021, with implementation of a 15% minimum tax set to begin soon in the EU, U.K., Japan, Indonesia and many other countries. The U.S. has notably not been able to pass this legislation.\nOur research suggests implementing this type of tax reform is necessary in reversing the shift of ever-greater amounts of corporate profits to tax havens—instead of being taxed by the governments where they operate and create value.", "53": "About a decade ago, the G-20, a forum of the world's biggest economies, agreed to crack down on multinational corporations' abusive use of tax havens. This resulted in a plan that aimed to curb practices that shielded a large chunk of corporate profits from tax authorities.\nBut, instead of reining in the use of tax havens—countries such as the Bahamas and Cayman Islands with very low or no effective tax rates—the problem has only gotten worse.\nIn a new study, we measured the excessive profits reported in tax havens that cannot be explained by ordinary economic activity such as employees, factories and research in that country. Our findings show a striking pattern of artificial shifting of paper profits to tax havens by corporations, which has been relentless since the 1980s.\nWe found that the world's biggest multinational businesses shifted 37% of the profits—$969 billion—they earned in other countries to tax havens in 2019. The figure was less than 2% in the 1970s. The main reasons for the large increase were the growth of the tax avoidance industry in the 1980s and U.S. policies that made it easier to shift profits from high-tax countries to tax havens.\nWe estimate that the amount of corporate taxes lost as a result reached 10% of total corporate revenue in 2019, up from less than 0.1% in the 1970s. In 2019, the total government tax loss globally was $250 billion. U.S. multinational corporations alone accounted for about half of that, followed by the U.K. and Germany.\nHow do policymakers fix this? So far, the world as a whole has been trying to solve this problem by cutting or scrapping corporate taxes in a very gradual way. In the past 40 years, the global effective corporate tax rate has fallen from 23% to 17%. At the same time, governments have relied more heavily on consumption taxes.\nBut the root cause of profit-shifting is the incentives involved, such as generous or lenient corporate tax rates in other countries. If countries could agree on a global minimum corporate tax rate of, say, 20%, the problem of profit-shifting would largely disappear, as tax havens would simply cease to exist.\nThis type of mechanism is exactly what more than 130 countries signed onto in 2021, with implementation of a 15% minimum tax set to begin soon in the EU, U.K., Japan, Indonesia and many other countries. The U.S. has notably not been able to pass this legislation.\nOur research suggests implementing this type of tax reform is necessary in reversing the shift of ever-greater amounts of corporate profits to tax havens—instead of being taxed by the governments where they operate and create value.", "54": "About a decade ago, the G-20, a forum of the world's biggest economies, agreed to crack down on multinational corporations' abusive use of tax havens. This resulted in a plan that aimed to curb practices that shielded a large chunk of corporate profits from tax authorities.\nBut, instead of reining in the use of tax havens—countries such as the Bahamas and Cayman Islands with very low or no effective tax rates—the problem has only gotten worse.\nIn a new study, we measured the excessive profits reported in tax havens that cannot be explained by ordinary economic activity such as employees, factories and research in that country. Our findings show a striking pattern of artificial shifting of paper profits to tax havens by corporations, which has been relentless since the 1980s.\nWe found that the world's biggest multinational businesses shifted 37% of the profits—$969 billion—they earned in other countries to tax havens in 2019. The figure was less than 2% in the 1970s. The main reasons for the large increase were the growth of the tax avoidance industry in the 1980s and U.S. policies that made it easier to shift profits from high-tax countries to tax havens.\nWe estimate that the amount of corporate taxes lost as a result reached 10% of total corporate revenue in 2019, up from less than 0.1% in the 1970s. In 2019, the total government tax loss globally was $250 billion. U.S. multinational corporations alone accounted for about half of that, followed by the U.K. and Germany.\nHow do policymakers fix this? So far, the world as a whole has been trying to solve this problem by cutting or scrapping corporate taxes in a very gradual way. In the past 40 years, the global effective corporate tax rate has fallen from 23% to 17%. At the same time, governments have relied more heavily on consumption taxes.\nBut the root cause of profit-shifting is the incentives involved, such as generous or lenient corporate tax rates in other countries. If countries could agree on a global minimum corporate tax rate of, say, 20%, the problem of profit-shifting would largely disappear, as tax havens would simply cease to exist.\nThis type of mechanism is exactly what more than 130 countries signed onto in 2021, with implementation of a 15% minimum tax set to begin soon in the EU, U.K., Japan, Indonesia and many other countries. The U.S. has notably not been able to pass this legislation.\nOur research suggests implementing this type of tax reform is necessary in reversing the shift of ever-greater amounts of corporate profits to tax havens—instead of being taxed by the governments where they operate and create value.", "55": "About a decade ago, the G-20, a forum of the world's biggest economies, agreed to crack down on multinational corporations' abusive use of tax havens. This resulted in a plan that aimed to curb practices that shielded a large chunk of corporate profits from tax authorities.\nBut, instead of reining in the use of tax havens—countries such as the Bahamas and Cayman Islands with very low or no effective tax rates—the problem has only gotten worse.\nIn a new study, we measured the excessive profits reported in tax havens that cannot be explained by ordinary economic activity such as employees, factories and research in that country. Our findings show a striking pattern of artificial shifting of paper profits to tax havens by corporations, which has been relentless since the 1980s.\nWe found that the world's biggest multinational businesses shifted 37% of the profits—$969 billion—they earned in other countries to tax havens in 2019. The figure was less than 2% in the 1970s. The main reasons for the large increase were the growth of the tax avoidance industry in the 1980s and U.S. policies that made it easier to shift profits from high-tax countries to tax havens.\nWe estimate that the amount of corporate taxes lost as a result reached 10% of total corporate revenue in 2019, up from less than 0.1% in the 1970s. In 2019, the total government tax loss globally was $250 billion. U.S. multinational corporations alone accounted for about half of that, followed by the U.K. and Germany.\nHow do policymakers fix this? So far, the world as a whole has been trying to solve this problem by cutting or scrapping corporate taxes in a very gradual way. In the past 40 years, the global effective corporate tax rate has fallen from 23% to 17%. At the same time, governments have relied more heavily on consumption taxes.\nBut the root cause of profit-shifting is the incentives involved, such as generous or lenient corporate tax rates in other countries. If countries could agree on a global minimum corporate tax rate of, say, 20%, the problem of profit-shifting would largely disappear, as tax havens would simply cease to exist.\nThis type of mechanism is exactly what more than 130 countries signed onto in 2021, with implementation of a 15% minimum tax set to begin soon in the EU, U.K., Japan, Indonesia and many other countries. The U.S. has notably not been able to pass this legislation.\nOur research suggests implementing this type of tax reform is necessary in reversing the shift of ever-greater amounts of corporate profits to tax havens—instead of being taxed by the governments where they operate and create value."}, "questions": [{"id": "2026-06-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What are the speakers mainly talking about?", "options": ["A) How to run one's business successfully.", "B) How to make progress in one's career.", "C) Why few people can fully display their capabilities.", "D) Why young people seldom get the expected promotion."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （主旨题） How to make progress in one's career.\n【定位】M: Welcome to \"Business Matters\". Today we have invited career development consultant\nLucy Williams to talk about how to get ahead at work. Hi, Lucy. Many of us never seem to\nget promoted. Why is that? And what can we do about it?\n男：欢迎来到《Business Matters》。今天我们邀请了职业发展顾问 Lucy Williams 来谈谈\n如何在职场上取得进步。嗨，Lucy。很多人似乎一直得不到晋升。为什么会这样？我们能做\n些什么？\n【信号】开头点题句。主持人开场即点明 “to talk about how to get ahead at work”，how to\nget ahead at work 直接给出对话主题；后续多次出现的 promoted、career 等词进一步\n锁定。\n【替换】how to get ahead at work ↔ How to make progress in one's career.\n同义改写：get ahead = make progress；at work = in one's career\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nHow to run one's business successfully. 中的 business 来自节目名 “Business\nMatters”，但对话讲的是职场晋升，不是如何经营自己的企业；属于用原文出现的\nbusiness 原词设陷阱。\n借用原文：Business Matters\nC) 过度推断\nWhy few people can fully display their capabilities. 中 “few people can fully display\ncapabilities” 在录音中并未出现，属于用 “never seem to get promoted” 过度推断出的伪\n主题。\nD) 原词陷阱\nWhy young people seldom get the expected promotion. 盗用了 Many of us never\nseem to get promoted. 中的 promoted，但全文讨论的是 “how to get ahead” 而不仅是\n“why young people don't get promoted”，属于以偏概全。\n借用原文：Many of us never seem to get promoted.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does the woman say is one mistake people make?", "options": ["A) To mix and interact with peers within the organization.", "B) To restrict their connections exclusively to colleagues.", "C) To limit communication only to their superiors.", "D) To underestimate their role in the company."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） To limit communication only to their superiors.\n【定位】W: One mistake people make is to limit communication only to their bosses. It's important to\nexpand your network and include your co-workers.\n女：人们犯的一个错误是只跟老板沟通。重要的是拓展人脉，把同事也包括进来。\n【信号】被问者直接应答 + 关键信息 “One mistake...”。男人在 P1 末尾问 “Why is that? And\nwhat can we do about it?”，女人在 P2 直接以 “One mistake people make is...” 作答，\nOne mistake people make is to limit communication only to their bosses. 就是答\n案句。\n【替换】limit communication only to their bosses. ↔ To limit communication only to their superiors.\n同义替换：bosses = superiors\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nTo mix and interact with peers within the organization. 中的 mix/interact with peers\n是女人后面建议 “expand your network and include your co-workers” 的同义改写，但\n题干问的是 “mistake”，这是正确做法而非错误。\n借用原文：expand your network and include your co-workers.\nB) 张冠李戴\nTo restrict their connections exclusively to colleagues. 盗用了 include your co-\nworkers. 中的 co-workers（colleagues 同义）与 communication/connections 概念，\n但把对象从 “bosses” 换成了 “colleagues”，与原文相反。\n借用原文：include your co-workers.\nD)\nTo underestimate their role in the company. 中的 underestimate 出现在 It's important\nnot to underestimate your colleagues.，但这是第4段关于“不要低估同事”的内容，与第2\n段的 “mistake” 不是同一个。\n借用原文：It's important not to underestimate your colleagues.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the woman advise people to do in their work?", "options": ["A) Put in quality time.", "B) Connect with managers.", "C) Make greater efforts.", "D) Demonstrate final results."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Put in quality time.\n【定位】W: Yeah, it's better to make sure you are putting in quality time instead of just a large quantity\nof time. And these efforts can easily be demonstrated by the results we achieve.\n女：是的，你最好确保自己投入的是高质量时间，而不是单纯的大量时间。并且这些努力可\n以通过我们取得的成果轻易体现出来。\n【信号】建议句 it's better to... + 取舍 instead of。男人在 P5 提出 “working hard... staying\nlate” 的困惑，女人在 P8 用 “it's better to...” 给出建议，并用 “instead of” 把 “quality\ntime” 与 “quantity of time” 对立，答案落在 quality time 上。\n【替换】putting in quality time ↔ Put in quality time.\n直接提取核心动作短语\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nConnect with managers. 对应 limit communication only to their bosses. 中的\nbosses，但女人在第2段明确说“只跟老板沟通”是错误，不是建议。\n借用原文：limit communication only to their bosses.\nC) 原词陷阱\nMake greater efforts. 对应男人在 working hard creates a good impression 中的\nworking hard，但女人随后否定 “constantly staying late just to show committed”，说\n明她不赞成单纯更努力。\n借用原文：working hard creates a good impression\nD) 偷换概念\nDemonstrate final results. 来自 these efforts can easily be demonstrated by the\nresults we achieve.，但原文中 results 是 quality time 的自然结果，不是女人直接建议去\n做的事。\n借用原文：these efforts can easily be demonstrated by the results we achieve.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "Why does the man say one should not act like a know-it-all?", "options": ["A) To show their genius.", "B) To gain due respect.", "C) To guard against humiliation.", "D) To avoid appearing arrogant."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） To avoid appearing arrogant.\n【定位】M: But it goes without saying that you shouldn't act like a know-it-all, otherwise you'll\nappear arrogant.\n男：但不用说，你不应该表现得像个万事通，否则你会显得傲慢。\n【信号】因果连接词 otherwise 后的结果。男人在 P11 用 “otherwise you'll appear arrogant” 直\n接给出 “不要表现得像 know-it-all” 的原因/后果，otherwise you'll appear arrogant. 是答\n案信号。\n【替换】you'll appear arrogant. ↔ To avoid appearing arrogant.\n条件-目的转换：otherwise you'll appear arrogant → to avoid appearing arrogant\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nTo show their genius. 来自 you'll get a lot of time later on to show your genius.，但\n那是 Lucy 对 “be humble” 的补充说明，不是 “不要 know-it-all” 的原因。\n借用原文：you'll get a lot of time later on to show your genius.\nB) 原词陷阱\nTo gain due respect. 来自 help them out to gain their respect and trust.，但这是关于\n帮助同事的建议，与 know-it-all 无关。\n借用原文：help them out to gain their respect and trust.\nC) 未提及\nTo guard against humiliation. 中的 humiliation 在录音中完全没有出现，是利用\narrogant 的负面情感色彩编造的近义干扰。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the woman say they first need to do?", "options": ["A) Decide how to divide up the presentation tasks.", "B) Investigate the available background research.", "C) Complete the project design together.", "D) Find a suitable topic for their article."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Decide how to divide up the presentation tasks.\n【定位】I think we first need to decide how to divide up the presentation responsibilities.\n我认为我们首先需要决定如何分配展示任务。\n【信号】first need to 给出动作起点。答案句以 first need to decide 开头，『first need to』是时\n间顺序与任务起点的双重信号，直接回答题干中的 first need to do。\n【替换】decide how to divide up the presentation responsibilities ↔\nDecide how to divide up the presentation tasks.\nresponsibilities 与 tasks 同义替换\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项 B 盗用原文原词 background research，但录音中 background research 是两人接下\n来讨论的分工对象，并非女士说的『first need to do』，属于答非所问。\n借用原文：background research\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 C 盗用 the project design，但 project design 只是展示的一个组成部分，不是女士\n说的 first need to do。\n借用原文：the project design\nD) 未提及 选项 D 的 find a suitable topic 在录音中完全没有对应内容，属于无中生有。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "Why does the man refuse to handle the background part?", "options": ["A) He wants to devote his time to other portions.", "B) He believes the woman is better able to handle it.", "C) He claims to have done most of the work already.", "D) He finds the woman more meticulous about details."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He claims to have done most of the work already.\n【定位】Hold on a minute. You want me, the one who did the bulk of the task, to also do the majority\nof the presentation? The background research which was so meticulous took more than\na month.\n等一下。你想让我这个做了大部分工作的人，还要做展示的大部分？那份非常细致的背景研\n究花了一个多月。\n【信号】反问语气+the one who 自我强调。男士用 Hold on a minute 打断，并以反问 You want\nme, the one who did the bulk of the task, to also do the majority of the\npresentation? 强调自己已经做了大量工作，这是拒绝的核心信号。\n【替换】the one who did the bulk of the task ↔ He claims to have done most of the work already.\nbulk of the task 与 most of the work 同义\n【排除】A) 答非所问\n男士后续确实承担了 project、conclusion 等其他部分，但这不是他拒绝 background 的\n原因；拒绝原因是『已经做了大部分工作』，A 属于因果倒置。\nB) 与原文相反\n男士强调的是『我做了大部分』，暗示女士做得少，而非认为女士更能胜任；B 与原文态度\n相反。\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 D 盗用录音原词 meticulous，但原文 meticulous 修饰的是 background research，\n不是 the woman，属于偷换修饰对象。\n借用原文：meticulous", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What does the woman urge the man to stop doing?", "options": ["A) Questioning.", "B) Complaining.", "C) Arguing.", "D) Worrying."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Complaining.\n【定位】OK. OK. I get it. Quit your moaning.\n好了好了，我明白了。别抱怨了。\n【信号】Quit your moaning 是直接劝阻。女士先说 OK. OK. I get it 表示理解，紧接着用祈使句\nQuit your moaning 直接要求男士停止某种行为，这是答案句的核心信号。\n【替换】Quit your moaning ↔ Complaining\nmoaning 与 complaining 同义\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 A 的 questioning 在录音中作为名词出现（question and answer），但并非女士让\n男士停止的行为，属于原词位置错配。\n借用原文：question and answer\nC) 偷换概念\n两人在讨论分工时确有分歧，但女士并没有让男士 stop arguing，而是具体针对他的抱怨\n说 Quit your moaning。\nD) 未提及 男士在抱怨而非担忧，录音中没有表达 worrying 的相关内容，属于无中生有。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "Why does the man suggest he present the conclusion and the woman do the introduction?", "options": ["A) To avoid sounding monotonous to the listeners.", "B) To make the division of labor more reasonable.", "C) To keep himself from being overburdened.", "D) To bring their capabilities fully into play."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） To avoid sounding monotonous to the listeners.\n【定位】I will take responsibility for the conclusion and put you in charge of the introduction.\nThis will make it so that we alternate presenting, so as to avoid sounding monotonous to\nthe audience.\n我负责结论部分，你负责开头部分。这样我们就会交替展示，以免让听众感到单调乏味。\n【信号】so as to 给出目的。男士先提出分工安排，然后用 This will make it so that...so as to... 给\n出目的，so as to 是典型的目的信号词，直接回答 why。\n【替换】avoid sounding monotonous to the audience ↔ To avoid sounding monotonous to the listeners.\naudience 与 listeners 同义\n【排除】B) 偷换概念\n整段对话确实在让分工更合理，但男士提出 conclusion/introduction 这一具体安排的目的\n不是『分工合理』，而是『避免单调』，B 属于过度泛化。\nC) 与原文相反\n男士主动承担 conclusion 和 project 等部分，并未表达怕自己负担过重；C 与对话中男士\n积极承担的态度不符。\nD) 未提及\n选项 D 的 capabilities fully into play 在录音中没有对应表述，属于用常见套话设置的无中\n生有项。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What can makeup do according to new research from the University of Stirling in Scotland?", "options": ["A) Alter others' perceptions of the wearer.", "B) Win others' admiration for the wearer.", "C) Enhance the wearer's confidence.", "D) Change the wearer's appearance."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Alter others' perceptions of the wearer.\n【定位】Makeup can do more than just change your appearance—it can also alter others' perceptions\nof you, for better or for worse. According to new research from the University of Stirling in\nScotland, although both men and women perceive women who wear makeup as being of a\nhigher status, men associate makeup with prestigious, positive qualities, while women view\nother women wearing makeup as being dominant and a potential threat.\n化妆不仅能改变你的外貌——它还能改变他人对你的看法，无论好坏。根据苏格兰斯特林大\n学的新研究，尽管男性和女性都认为化妆的女性地位更高，但男性将化妆与有声望的积极品\n质联系在一起，而女性则认为化妆的其他女性更具主导性且是一种潜在威胁。\n【信号】主题句 + 研究来源句。答案信号来自段落开头的主题句 'Makeup can do more than just\nchange your appearance—it can also alter others' perceptions of you'，其中 'it can\nalso alter others' perceptions of you' 直接回应题干 'What can makeup do'；随后\n'According to new research from the University of Stirling in Scotland' 进一步给出具\n体研究发现。\n【替换】alter others' perceptions of you ↔ Alter others' perceptions of the wearer.\n同义改写：第二人称转第三人称\n【排除】B) 答非所问\n选项 B 的 admiration 看似和 gain others' trust and admiration 有关，但该句是作者基\n于研究给出的应用建议，并非题干所问的 'new research from the University of Stirling'\n的直接结论，属于答非所问。\n借用原文：gain others' trust and admiration\nC) 未提及 选项 C 的 confidence 在全部四段录音中均无对应内容，属于未提及。\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 D 盗用原文原词 change your appearance，但原文说 'Makeup can do more than\njust change your appearance'，言下之意 '改变外貌' 只是常识层面，新研究真正关注的是\n'more than' 之后的 'alter others' perceptions'；选项把 'more than just' 之前的旧信息拿\n来当答案，答非所问。\n借用原文：change your appearance", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "How did both men and women rate the faces with cosmetics in the new research?", "options": ["A) As being a greater threat.", "B) As being of higher prestige.", "C) As being more dominant.", "D) As being more attractive."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） As being more attractive.\n【定位】The research showed that men and women both rated the faces with cosmetics as being more\nattractive, but only women rated made-up faces as being more dominant, while only men\nrated made-up faces as being higher in prestige.\n研究显示，男性和女性都认为化妆后的脸更具吸引力，但只有女性认为化妆后的脸更具主导\n性，只有男性认为化妆后的脸更有声望。\n【信号】对比结构 'both...but only...while only...'。答案信号是对比结构 'men and women\nboth... but only women... while only men...'，题干问 'both men and women'，因此答\n案落在 'both' 引导的分句里，即 'more attractive'。\n【替换】men and women both rated the faces with cosmetics as being more attractive ↔\nAs being more attractive.\n直接对应：原句谓语改写为选项表语\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n选项 A 的 threat 来自 a potential threat，但该评价是女性对其他化妆女性的看法，不是男\n女共同看法；且选项把 'a potential threat' 改写为 'a greater threat'，也有程度偷换之\n嫌。\n借用原文：a potential threat\nB) 偷换概念\n选项 B 的 prestige 来自 higher in prestige，但原文明确说 'only men rated made-up\nfaces as being higher in prestige'，不是 both men and women。\n借用原文：higher in prestige\nC) 偷换概念\n选项 C 的 dominant 来自 more dominant，但原文明确 'only women rated made-up\nfaces as being more dominant'，不是 both men and women。\n借用原文：more dominant", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "Why does a female candidate need to know the gender of the hiring committee members?", "options": ["A) It might influence her way of introducing herself.", "B) It might impact her dressing style for the occasion.", "C) It might affect her decision on applying cosmetics.", "D) It might sway her view on the interviewers' questions."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It might affect her decision on applying cosmetics.\n【定位】At a job interview, knowing whether the hiring committee will consist of men or women\nmight influence a female candidate's decision about wearing makeup.\n在工作面试中，知道招聘委员会将由男性还是女性组成可能会影响女性候选人是否化妆的\n决定。\n【信号】应用举例中的 'might influence... decision about wearing makeup'。答案信号来自段\n落中的应用举例句：'At a job interview, knowing whether... might influence a female\ncandidate's decision about wearing makeup'，其中 'knowing whether the hiring\ncommittee will consist of men or women' 直接对应题干的 'know the gender of the\nhiring committee members'，'might influence... decision about wearing makeup' 直\n接回答 why。\n【替换】influence a female candidate's decision about wearing makeup ↔\nIt might affect her decision on applying cosmetics.\n同义改写：动词、名词、人称三处对应\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项 A 的 'way of introducing herself' 在录音中完全找不到对应内容，属于未提及。\nB) 偷换概念\n选项 B 的 dressing style 在录音中未出现；录音只讨论 wearing makeup / cosmetics，未\n涉及服装，属于用形象管理大类偷换具体对象。\nD) 偷换概念\n选项 D 的 'view on the interviewers' questions' 在录音中没有对应内容；原文 the\ninterviewers will view her as attractive, dominant and prestigious 说的是面试官如何看\n待她，而不是她如何看待面试官的问题，属于偷换主客体。\n借用原文：the interviewers will view her as attractive, dominant and prestigious", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What methods do some advertisements trumpet about changing a habit?", "options": ["A) Those involving no self-recognition.", "B) Those forcing us to clench our teeth.", "C) Those resisting various temptations.", "D) Those requiring no willpower at all."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Those requiring no willpower at all.\n【定位】You may have seen advertisements trumpeting methods of changing a habit that require\nabsolutely no willpower whatsoever.\n你可能见过一些广告，大肆宣扬改变习惯的方法完全不需要任何意志力。\n【信号】开篇直接陈述广告内容。录音开头用 advertisements trumpeting methods of changing\na habit that require absolutely no willpower whatsoever 直接抛出广告的说法，随后才\n设问反驳，故首句即答案句。\n【替换】require absolutely no willpower whatsoever ↔ Those requiring no willpower at all.\n同义替换：absolutely no ... whatsoever = no ... at all\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项 Those involving no self-recognition. 中的 self-recognition 在录音中完全没有出\n现，属于无中生有。\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项 Those forcing us to clench our teeth. 盗用了原文 the ability to clench their teeth\n中的原词 clench their teeth，但原文说的是“大多数人认为意志力就是咬紧牙关”，并非广\n告吹嘘的方法。\n借用原文：the ability to clench their teeth\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Those resisting various temptations. 盗用了原文 resist attractive temptations 中\n的 resist temptations，但原文同样是人们对 willpower 的误解，不是广告内容。\n借用原文：resist attractive temptations", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the speaker define willpower as?", "options": ["A) Regulating one's actions for the sake of good health.", "B) Controlling one's actions with a motivating thought.", "C) Using practical examples to guide one's behavior.", "D) Denying oneself any comfort to prevent diseases."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Controlling one's actions with a motivating thought.\n【定位】I define willpower as \"using only the thought of your motivators to control your actions.\"\n我把意志力定义为“只用你的动力念头来控制你的行为”。\n【信号】定义句 I define ... as。原文用 I define willpower as 明确给出说话者的定义，其后引号内\n容即为答案。\n【替换】using only the thought of your motivators to control your actions ↔\nControlling one's actions with a motivating thought.\n同义替换：thought of your motivators = motivating thought；control your actions =\ncontrolling one's actions\n【排除】A)\n选项 Regulating one's actions for the sake of good health. 中的 good health 是原文\nprevent diabetes、reach a healthy weight 等具体 motivator 的概括，但说话者定义的是\n“用动力念头控制行为”，而非为了健康。\n借用原文：prevent diabetes\nC)\n选项 Using practical examples to guide one's behavior. 中的 practical examples 看似\n对应原文 Let's say your motivators are \"prevent diabetes\" and \"reach a healthy\nweight\" 的举例，但举例是用来解释定义的，不是定义本身。\n借用原文：Let's say your motivators are \"prevent diabetes\" and \"reach a healthy weight\"\nD)\n选项 Denying oneself any comfort to prevent diseases. 中的 denying oneself any\ncomfort 对应原文 willpower means self-denial and no fun，但这是大众看法，且\nprevent diseases 把 diabetes 过度概括为 diseases。\n借用原文：willpower means self-denial and no fun", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What may sound amazing enough as regards willpower?", "options": ["A) It equips us with an expert skill.", "B) It exerts an enduring force on us.", "C) It leads to a burning goal.", "D) It begins with a bright idea."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It begins with a bright idea.\n【定位】So, amazingly enough, willpower begins with a thought—a bright idea—some way to\nimprove your life or contribute to others.\n因此，令人惊讶的是，意志力始于一个念头——一个绝妙的想法——某种改善你生活或帮助\n他人的方式。\n【信号】评价副词 amazingly enough + 结论动词 begins with。原文用 amazingly enough,\nwillpower begins with a thought 直接回应题干的 sound amazing，其后 a bright idea\n是同位解释。\n【替换】willpower begins with a thought—a bright idea ↔ It begins with a bright idea.\n同义替换：a thought—a bright idea 概括为 a bright idea\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 It equips us with an expert skill. 中的 expert 盗用了原文 Doctor Walter Mischel,\nan expert on willpower skills 中的 expert / skills，但原文说的是 Mischel 是研究\nwillpower skills 的专家，而非 willpower 给我们 expert skill。\n借用原文：Doctor Walter Mischel, an expert on willpower skills\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项 It exerts an enduring force on us. 盗用了原文 this idea is the force behind your\nwillpower 中的 force，但原文说“想法是 willpower 背后的力量”，不是说 willpower 对我\n们施加 enduring force。\n借用原文：this idea is the force behind your willpower\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 It leads to a burning goal. 盗用了原文 a burning goal，但原文说“无论你是把\nmotivator 叫作 burning goal 还是 bright idea，它都是 willpower 的推动力”，即\nburning goal 是 motivator 的别名，不是 willpower 导致的结果。\n借用原文：a burning goal", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the speaker say may make for effective willpower?", "options": ["A) Any cherished goal.", "B) Any positive mindset.", "C) Any personal instinct.", "D) Any valued role model."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Any cherished goal.\n【定位】What sort of bright ideas—motivators—make for effective willpower? In fact, any goal that\nyou value strongly counts.\n什么样的绝妙想法——动力——能造就有效的意志力？事实上，任何你强烈珍视的目标都\n算数。\n【信号】设问自答 + in fact 强调。原文先用 What sort of bright ideas—motivators—make for\neffective willpower? 抛出题干，再用 In fact, any goal that you value strongly counts\n给出答案，in fact 是答案信号。\n【替换】any goal that you value strongly counts ↔ Any cherished goal.\n同义替换：value strongly = cherished；goal 直接对应\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项 Any positive mindset. 中的 positive 盗用了原文 drive a positive change 中的\npositive，但原文说的是 positive change，不是 positive mindset。\n借用原文：drive a positive change\nC) 未提及\n选项 Any personal instinct. 中的 instinct 在录音中完全没有提及，且与说话者强调“用\nmotivator 控制行为”的方向相反。\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Any valued role model. 中的 valued 来自原文 you value strongly，role model 来\n自 being a role model for your child，但原文把 role model 仅作为 goal 的一个例子，\n且 valued 修饰的是 goal，不是 role model。\n借用原文：being a role model for your child", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the speaker say about the federal government's advice on seafood safety?", "options": ["A) It has long been criticized as unclear.", "B) It has long been ignored by the public.", "C) It has caused much panic among women.", "D) It has resulted in different understandings."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It has long been criticized as unclear.\n【定位】In part, that's because the federal government's advice on the matter, first issued in 2004, has\nlong been criticized as vague.\n部分原因在于，联邦政府关于这一问题的建议（2004年首次发布）长期以来一直被批评为含\n糊不清。\n【信号】篇章首段点题+评价词vague。录音开篇先讲孕妇的困扰，随后用the federal\ngovernment's advice...has long been criticized as vague直接点出建议的评价，正是题\n干所问。\n【替换】has long been criticized as vague ↔ It has long been criticized as unclear.\nvague=unclear同义替换\nthe federal government's advice ↔ It\n机构名词用代词回指\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音说建议has long been criticized（被批评），而It has long been ignored by the\npublic.把动词换成ignored，且把对象泛化为the public，与原文不符。\n借用原文：has long been criticized\nC) 答非所问\n录音确实提到scaring many pregnant and breastfeeding women away from eating\nseafood altogether，但这是critics对advisory后果的补充，并非题干问的speaker对\nadvice本身的直接描述；且panic among women把'被吓得不吃了'夸大为'恐慌'。\n借用原文：scaring many pregnant and breastfeeding women away from eating seafood altogether\nD) 张冠李戴\nso it confuses everyone讲的是critics对new advice的看法，而题干问的是federal\ngovernment's advice（2004年起的老建议）；It has resulted in different\nunderstandings.用different understandings对应confuses everyone，属于时间/对象错\n位。\n借用原文：so it confuses everyone", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What did the Food and Drug Administration do this week?", "options": ["A) It warned consumers not to eat fish from the Gulf of Mexico.", "B) It called on expectant mothers to pay attention to food safety.", "C) It issued an update to the advice on seafood consumption.", "D) It listed more than sixty species of fish as \"choices to avoid\"."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It issued an update to the advice on seafood consumption.\n【定位】This week the FDA issued an update to the advice, listing more than sixty species in a chart\nthat ranks fish as A Best Choice, Good Choice and Choices to Avoid.\n本周FDA发布了对该建议的更新，列出60多种鱼类，并按'最佳选择'、'良好选择'和'应避免\n选择'进行分级。\n【信号】时间信号this week+动作issued。题干问this week FDA做了什么，录音用This week\nthe FDA issued an update to the advice直接给出答案，this week与issued构成强定位\n信号。\n【替换】the FDA issued an update to the advice ↔\nIt issued an update to the advice on seafood consumption.\n动作短语原词复现+主题范畴概括\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n录音说的是expectant mothers have long been warned to stay away from shark and\nfour other particular types of fish from the Gulf of Mexico，主语是expectant\nmothers而非consumers，对象是shark and four other particular types而非全部fish\nfrom the Gulf；It warned consumers not to eat fish from the Gulf of Mexico.把范围扩\n大、主体替换。\n借用原文：expectant mothers have long been warned to stay away from shark and four other\nparticular types of fish from the Gulf of Mexico\nB) 偷换概念\n录音说新建议的目标是make it easier for mothers-to-be to feel confident about the\ntype of seafood they include in their diets，并非call on them to pay attention；It\ncalled on expectant mothers to pay attention to food safety.把目的改成了号召，且\nfood safety范围过宽。\n借用原文：make it easier for mothers-to-be to feel confident about the type of seafood they include\nin their diets\nD) 偷换概念\n录音说listing more than sixty species in a chart that ranks fish as A Best Choice,\nGood Choice and Choices to Avoid，即分为三类；It listed more than sixty species of\nfish as \"choices to avoid\".只取其中一类，把整体分级偷换成全部列为应避免。\n借用原文：listing more than sixty species in a chart that ranks fish as A Best Choice, Good Choice\nand Choices to Avoid", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "According to the speaker, what are people concerned about regarding pregnant women and seafood?", "options": ["A) The impact of the FDA's analysis.", "B) Multiple factors including mercury.", "C) Critics' conclusions.", "D) The prices of seafood."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Multiple factors including mercury.\n【定位】While mercury is of great concern when people are talking about pregnant women and\nseafood, it's not the only factor contributing to anxiety.\n当人们谈论孕妇与海鲜时，汞固然备受关注，但它并非造成焦虑的唯一因素。\n【信号】让步while+否定not the only factor。录音先用mercury is of great concern让步，再用\nit's not the only factor contributing to anxiety转折强调'还有其他因素'，正好对应题干\nconcerned about的内容。\n【替换】it's not the only factor contributing to anxiety ↔ Multiple factors including mercury.\nnot the only factor → multiple factors 同义替换\nmercury is of great concern ↔ including mercury\nmercury原词保留\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音中虽提到FDA analyzed fish consumption data，但从未说人们担忧的是The impact\nof the FDA's analysis.；该选项用原文词FDA/analysis拼凑，属于未提及。\nC) 张冠李戴\n录音中Critics say the new advice doesn't always agree with the agency's own\nscientific findings.是critics对new advice的质疑，而题干问的是people对pregnant\nwomen and seafood的concerned about，对象不同。\n借用原文：Critics say the new advice doesn't always agree with the agency's own\nscientific findings.\nD)\n全文没有任何地方讨论seafood prices，The prices of seafood.是利用常识（海鲜价格）\n设下的无中生有项。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the speaker say about people not taking any vacation?", "options": ["A) They will suffer a breakdown soon.", "B) They will be losing both joy and life.", "C) They will be putting themselves and family at risk.", "D) They will make things worse for people around them."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They will be losing both joy and life.\n【定位】One 9-year-long study published in Psychosomatic Medicine suggests not taking at least one\nvacation a year may increase your risk of mortality due to heart disease. So, not only are you\nkilling your joy, you're possibly killing yourself.\n《心理医学》发表的一项长达9年的研究表明，每年至少不休假一次可能会增加死于心脏病\n的风险。所以，你不仅扼杀了自己的快乐，还可能葬送自己的性命。\n【信号】研究结论 + so 递进。录音先用 One 9-year-long study suggests... 抛出研究结论，再用\nSo, not only are you killing your joy, you're possibly killing yourself 递进强调后果，其\n中 not only...but（also）结构把 joy 和 life（killing yourself）并置，正是答案句。\n【替换】not only are you killing your joy, you're possibly killing yourself ↔\nThey will be losing both joy and life\n同义替换：killing joy → losing joy；possibly killing yourself → losing life\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音从未出现 breakdown；P1 只说 the worse you're making things for yourself，指\n“让自己情况更糟”，并非 suffer a breakdown soon。\nC) 偷换概念\n录音说 increase your risk of mortality due to heart disease 与 killing yourself，风险对\n象是自己；选项加入 family，把原句中的 risk 曲解为“自己和家人”。\n借用原文：may increase your risk of mortality due to heart disease\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音 the worse you're making things for yourself 说的是“让自己情况更糟”，选项把对象\n改为 people around them，制造了“影响周围人”的错觉。\n借用原文：the worse you're making things for yourself", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What does the 9-year study recommend people do when planning vacations?", "options": ["A) Spend 14 days for vacation every year.", "B) Aim for three separate vacations yearly.", "C) Space out their vacations evenly instead of taking one long vacation.", "D) Use all their days off for a long summer break to achieve full-on calm."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Space out their vacations evenly instead of taking one long vacation.\n【定位】That above-mentioned study also recommends you space out your vacations evenly instead of\nusing up all of your days at once.\n上述研究还建议你不要把假期一次性用完，而是均匀地分散开来安排。\n【信号】recommends + instead of 建议结构。录音中 recommends you space out your\nvacations evenly instead of using up all of your days at once 直接给出研究建议，\nrecommends 与 instead of 的对照标志答案落点。\n【替换】space out your vacations evenly instead of using up all of your days at once ↔\nSpace out their vacations evenly instead of taking one long vacation\n同义改写：space out evenly 直接对应；using up all days at once 转述为 taking one long\nvacation\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音 If you have 14 days of paid leave, that's almost three separate 8-day vacations...\n只是假设举例，并非建议“每年必须休14天”。\n借用原文：14 days of paid leave\nB) 偷换概念\n录音说如果只有14天带薪假“几乎可以分成三次8天假期”，这是基于假设的算法，不是研究\n对所有人的统一建议。\n借用原文：almost three separate 8-day vacations you can use to split your year into thirds\nD) 与原文相反\n录音 instead of using up all of your days at once 明确否定一次性休完，且 As tempting\nas it might be to use all your days to take one long summer break 也指出这是\ntemptation，D 项把它当成建议。\n借用原文：using up all of your days at once", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "Why is vacation frequency important according to the speaker?", "options": ["A) Expecting to have more vacations in a year will boost one's work efficiency.", "B) Anticipating more vacations would reduce one's feelings of despair at work.", "C) Taking more than three vacations yearly would prolong one's excitement.", "D) Planning multiple breaks throughout the year will add to one's happiness."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Planning multiple breaks throughout the year will add to one's happiness.\n【定位】Vacation frequency is important because planning multiple breaks throughout the year means\nyou always have something to look forward to. Planning things and the anticipation that\ncomes with it tend to make us happier.\n休假频率之所以重要，是因为一年中计划多次休息意味着你总有盼头。计划事情以及随之而\n来的期待往往会让我们更快乐。\n【信号】because 直接解释原因 + 评价词 happier。录音先以 Vacation frequency is important\nbecause planning multiple breaks throughout the year means you always have\nsomething to look forward to 点明因果，再用 the anticipation that comes with it\ntend to make us happier 给出积极评价，because 后内容即答案。\n【替换】planning multiple breaks throughout the year means you always have something to look forward to\n↔ Planning multiple breaks throughout the year will add to one's happiness\n同义改写：planning multiple breaks throughout the year 原样对应；have something to look\nforward to + make us happier → add to one's happiness\nthe anticipation that comes with it tend to make us happier ↔\nPlanning multiple breaks throughout the year will add to one's happiness\n同义改写：anticipation/tend to make us happier 对应 add to one's happiness\n【排除】A) 未提及 录音讨论的是期待、快乐、despair，从未提到工作效率，属于无中生有。\nB) 偷换概念\n录音说如果一年只休一次长假，兴奋感太远才会 lead to feelings of despair；选项把“减少\n绝望”说成是期待更多休假的好处，但原文只用它衬托多次休假的好处，且对象也有偏差。\n借用原文：it can actually lead to feelings of despair\nC) 过度推断\n录音未说“超过三次”能延长兴奋；P4 的 three separate vacations 只是 14 天假期的算法举\n例，P5 的 excitement builds much more slowly 是批评一年一次，不是支持三次以上。\n借用原文：three separate 8-day vacations you can use to split your year into thirds", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What are researchers doing in Sweden?", "options": ["A) Surveying when drivers are caught in the heat of arguments.", "B) Designing devices to prevent drivers from being distracted.", "C) Testing models for monitoring drivers easily getting tired.", "D) Developing sensor-based systems for autonomous vehicles."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Developing sensor-based systems for autonomous vehicles.\n【定位】Researchers working as part of the European Union's €77 billion research program are\ndeveloping sensor-based systems that monitor whether a driver is distracted, anxious or tired.\n作为欧盟 770 亿欧元研究项目的一部分，研究人员正在开发传感系统，用以监测驾驶员是否\n分心、焦虑或疲劳。\n【信号】题干关键词原词复现+直接动作句。题干中的 researchers 与 doing 在 Researchers\nworking as part of the European Union's €77 billion research program are\ndeveloping sensor-based systems 中原词出现，developing 直接回答“做什么”，因此该\n句即为答案句。\n【替换】developing sensor-based systems that monitor whether a driver is distracted, anxious or tired ↔\nDeveloping sensor-based systems for autonomous vehicles.\n核心动宾原词复现+目的同义\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Surveying when drivers are caught in the heat of arguments. 中的 caught in the\nheat of arguments 在 caught in the heat of an argument 中确实出现，但原文是说自动\n驾驶汽车在驾驶员情绪激动时接管，而非 researchers 在 survey/调查，动作被换。\n借用原文：caught in the heat of an argument\nB) 偷换概念\n选项 Designing devices to prevent drivers from being distracted. 用了原文 distracted\n和 prevent accidents 两个词，但原文传感器是监测 distraction 并防止事故，不是 design\ndevices 专门 prevent distraction。\n借用原文：distracted\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Testing models for monitoring drivers easily getting tired. 用了 tired，但原文是\ndeveloping sensor-based systems，不是 testing models；monitoring 是系统功能，不\n是测试模型。\n借用原文：tired", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What did the researchers' project aim to do according to Dr. Anna Anund?", "options": ["A) Make sure the autonomous vehicle operates in the simplest manner.", "B) Ensure the transition from the driver to the vehicle is safely made.", "C) Alleviate the driver's pressure as much as possible.", "D) Record the driver's state as accurately as possible."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （目的题） Ensure the transition from the driver to the vehicle is safely made.\n【定位】She said the aim was to really make sure that the transition from the driver to the vehicle is\ndone in a safe way, so we can take into consideration the driver's state, and so we do not give\ncontrol to a driver who is under pressure of fatigue, stress, anxiety, and so on.\n她说，项目的目标是真正确保从驾驶员到车辆的接管过程以安全方式完成，从而把驾驶员状\n态纳入考量，避免把控制权交给处于疲劳、压力、焦虑等状态下的驾驶员。\n【信号】the aim was to 目的信号+make sure 强调。the aim was to really make sure that the\ntransition from the driver to the vehicle is done in a safe way 中，the aim was to 直\n接对应题干 aim，make sure 强调目标，其后内容即为答案。\n【替换】the transition from the driver to the vehicle is done in a safe way ↔\nEnsure the transition from the driver to the vehicle is safely made.\n同义改写+主被动转换\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项 Make sure the autonomous vehicle operates in the simplest manner. 中的\nsimplest 在原文未出现，且把 “safe” 偷换为 “simplest”。\nC) 偷换概念\n选项 Alleviate the driver's pressure as much as possible. 盗用 under pressure of\nfatigue, stress, anxiety, and so on，但原文是说不要把车交给有压力的驾驶员，而不是以\n“减轻压力”为项目目的。\n借用原文：under pressure of fatigue, stress, anxiety, and so on\nD) 答非所问\n选项 Record the driver's state as accurately as possible. 对应 a driver's state can be\nmeasured using a number of sensors, including heart rate monitors and eye\nsensors, which track where a driver's gaze is，但 measuring the state 是实现安全接管\n的手段，不是项目 aim。\n借用原文：a driver's state can be measured using a number of sensors, including heart rate\nmonitors and eye sensors, which track where a driver's gaze is", "points": ["听力·讲座", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What is thought to be responsible for a fifth of road accidents across the European Union?", "options": ["A) Driver fatigue.", "B) Dreadful traffic.", "C) Drunk driving.", "D) Driving time."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Driver fatigue.\n【定位】Driver fatigue is thought to be responsible for as many as a fifth of road crashes across the\nEU, although there are certain times of the day when more accidents happen.\n据认为，驾驶员疲劳导致了欧盟多达五分之一的道路撞车事故，尽管一天中某些时段事故\n更多。\n【信号】题干关键词原词复现+主语即答案。responsible for as many as a fifth of road crashes\nacross the EU 与题干 responsible for a fifth of road accidents across the European\nUnion 几乎原词对应，其主语 Driver fatigue 就是答案。\n【替换】Driver fatigue is thought to be responsible for as many as a fifth of road crashes across the EU ↔\nDriver fatigue.\n主语直接对应，其余为题干已知信息\n【排除】B) 未提及\n选项 Dreadful traffic. 是交通事故的常见原因，但录音原文从未出现 traffic 相关表述。\nC) 未提及\n选项 Drunk driving. 同样是常见事故原因，但原文没有 drunk/drinking/alcohol 等信息。\nD) 偷换概念\n选项 Driving time. 对应 there are certain times of the day when more accidents\nhappen，但原文只说某些时段事故更多，并未把 driving time 当作 responsible for a\nfifth of accidents 的原因。\n借用原文：there are certain times of the day when more accidents happen", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "Why do drivers now view self-driving cars as less safe than they used to?", "options": ["A) There has been a strong debate on the feasibility of Level 3 cars.", "B) There have been few remarkable advances in auto technology.", "C) There has been a rise in attention-grabbing accidents recently.", "D) There have been few feasible ways to shift public sentiment."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） There has been a rise in attention-grabbing accidents recently.\n【定位】With an increase in attention-grabbing accidents recently, drivers now view self-driving cars\nas less safe than they used to.\n随着近期引人注目的交通事故增多，驾驶员现在认为自动驾驶汽车不如以前安全。\n【信号】With 引导原因状语，直接解释结果。With an increase in attention-grabbing accidents\nrecently 位于结果句 “drivers now view self-driving cars as less safe than they used to”\n之前，with 短语说明原因。\n【替换】an increase in attention-grabbing accidents recently ↔\nThere has been a rise in attention-grabbing accidents recently.\n同义替换：increase→rise\n【排除】A) 答非所问\n选项 There has been a strong debate on the feasibility of Level 3 cars. 对应 there is a\nstrong debate going on about how it is possible to make a Level 3 car completely\nsafe.，但这是在讨论技术可行性，不是 drivers 觉得更不安全的原因。\n借用原文：there is a strong debate going on about how it is possible to make a Level 3 car\ncompletely safe.\nB) 与原文相反\n选项 There have been few remarkable advances in auto technology. 与 Despite\nadvances in technology 相反，原文明确说技术有进步。\n借用原文：Despite advances in technology\nD) 偷换概念\n选项 There have been few feasible ways to shift public sentiment. 盗用 shifting\npublic sentiment，但原文是说我们在转变公众情绪方面还有路要走，并非说缺乏可行方\n法，也不是驾驶员态度变化的原因。\n借用原文：we still have some way to go in shifting public sentiment.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) anywhere", "B) asserts", "C) constituent", "D) correlated", "E) determining", "F) devastating", "G) enacted", "H) entails", "I) exemption", "J) impose", "K) notified", "L) notwithstanding", "M) presume", "N) rehabilitation", "O) reproaching"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) impose\n动词\n【词性槽】复数主语 proposals 之后缺谓语，需动词原形（一般现在时）\n【依据】impose caps/limits on 是政策语域的固定搭配，「对……设限」\nThe proposals __(26)__ caps on rent increases, preventing landlords in the state from\nraising rent more than the rate of inflation in most cases.——提案「对租金涨幅设上\n限」，呼应首段 put some limits on ever-increasing rent prices，impose caps on 与\nput limits on 是同义改写，这正是六级最爱考的「换说法不换意思」。\n【竞争词】M) presume presume 意为「假定」，与 caps 不构成搭配且逻辑相反\npresume 是「假定、推测」，presume caps 不是英语搭配；且提案是「实际设限」而非「假定\n存在上限」，代入后与后半句 preventing landlords…（实际阻止房东涨租）矛盾。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 M) —— presume 与 impose 同为原形动词，「提案假定上限」粗\n读似乎也通。The proposals __(26)__ caps on rent increases 中 presume 形态合格，且\n考生若只认识 presume 不认识 impose，容易用「熟悉的词」填。防坑：盯住宾语搭配\n——caps/limits 的舞伴是 impose（impose caps on），presume 后接的是「事实从\n句」；再回看首段 put some limits on ever-increasing rent prices，同义改写的锚点直接\n指向 impose。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) anywhere", "B) asserts", "C) constituent", "D) correlated", "E) determining", "F) devastating", "G) enacted", "H) entails", "I) exemption", "J) impose", "K) notified", "L) notwithstanding", "M) presume", "N) rehabilitation", "O) reproaching"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) exemption\n名词\n【词性槽】不定冠词 a + 名词作定语的 hardship 之后，需单数可数名词\n【依据】hardship exemption「困难豁免」是政策文本的固定说法，且与 exceptions 同义呼应\nThere are exceptions for buildings less than 10 years old, public housing,\nimprovements beyond maintenance, and a hardship __(27)__.——列举的都是「不适用\n该限制的豁免情形」，a hardship exemption（因困难而获得的豁免）与句首 exceptions\n同义复现，政策语域用词精准。\n【竞争词】C) constituent constituent「选民」不能与 hardship 构成政策豁免概念\na hardship constituent 字面上是「一位困难的选民」，但本句列举的是例外「情形/类别」\n（建筑、公屋、改良工程），不是人；且选民不属于租金管制条例的豁免条款范畴，语义场\n不合。\nN) rehabilitation rehabilitation 意为「康复/修复」，与 hardship 搭配不成立\nhardship rehabilitation（困难修复）不是英语里的政策术语；rehabilitation 多指罪犯改造、\n病人康复或建筑翻修，与「豁免条款」的语境无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) anywhere", "B) asserts", "C) constituent", "D) correlated", "E) determining", "F) devastating", "G) enacted", "H) entails", "I) exemption", "J) impose", "K) notified", "L) notwithstanding", "M) presume", "N) rehabilitation", "O) reproaching"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) correlated\n动词\n【词性槽】be 动词 is 之后、介词 to 之前，需过去分词构成 be done 表语结构\n【依据】be correlated to 是固定搭配，意为「与……相关」，严丝合缝\n研究语境 researchers at the U.S. Government Accountability Office estimated that\nevery $100 in rent increase is __(28)__ to a 9% rise in homelessness：租金每涨 100 美\n元「对应/关联」无家可归率上升 9%——这正是相关性表述，be correlated to/with 是六\n级阅读与选词填空的高频固定搭配，语义与搭配双契合。\n【竞争词】G) enacted enact 的搭配对象是法律而非数据关系\nenacted 意为「颁布（法律）」，其主语应是立法机构、宾语是法案；「租金上涨被颁布成无家\n可归率上升 9%」语义不通，且 enact 不与 to 构成表语搭配。\nK) notified notify 的对象必须是人\nnotify sb of/that… 意为「通知某人」，被通知方是人；这里主语是 every $100 in rent\nincrease（物），「租金上涨被通知给无家可归率」不成立，也不与 to 搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) anywhere", "B) asserts", "C) constituent", "D) correlated", "E) determining", "F) devastating", "G) enacted", "H) entails", "I) exemption", "J) impose", "K) notified", "L) notwithstanding", "M) presume", "N) rehabilitation", "O) reproaching"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) constituent\n名词\n【词性槽】不定冠词 a 之后、分词短语 struggling… 之前，需单数可数名词指人\n【依据】议员讲「选民」的艰难故事，身份与语境严丝合缝\nRepresentative Alex Ramel explained why he thought that this kind of legislation\nwas so important, telling the story of a __(29)__ struggling to live in the community.\n——说话者是 Representative（众议员），议员为自己的立法辩护时讲的是「选区选民」\n的故事；constituent 正是「（选区的）选民」，且下一段 he made the decision to\nintroduce the legislation while talking to a young man in Bellingham 的 a young\nman 就是这个 constituent 的具体化。\n【竞争词】N) rehabilitation rehabilitation 是抽象事物名词，不能「挣扎着生活」\nrehabilitation（康复/修复）是物不是人，无法充当 struggling to live 的逻辑主语；「一个挣\n扎着住在社区里的康复」语义荒谬，直接排除。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) anywhere", "B) asserts", "C) constituent", "D) correlated", "E) determining", "F) devastating", "G) enacted", "H) entails", "I) exemption", "J) impose", "K) notified", "L) notwithstanding", "M) presume", "N) rehabilitation", "O) reproaching"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) devastating\n形容词\n【词性槽】定冠词 the 与名词 effect 之间，需形容词作定语\n【依据】devastating effect 是高频搭配，且与「两位数涨幅」的负面语境吻合\n上下文 Ramel pointed out the __(30)__ effect of double-digit rent increases 与 Rent\nincreases have an even more detrimental effect on some demographic groups 呼\n应：说话者是立法支持者，强调租金暴涨的「毁灭性」影响，devastating effect（毁灭性\n的影响）搭配与感情色彩双双贴合。\n【竞争词】C) constituent constituent 作形容词意为「组成的」，与 effect 不搭\nconstituent 虽有形容词用法（组成的、构成整体的），但 constituent effect 不是英语搭配；\n其名词义「选民」也与本空语法槽（名词前的定语形容词）语义不合。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) anywhere", "B) asserts", "C) constituent", "D) correlated", "E) determining", "F) devastating", "G) enacted", "H) entails", "I) exemption", "J) impose", "K) notified", "L) notwithstanding", "M) presume", "N) rehabilitation", "O) reproaching"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) notified\n动词\n【词性槽】his landlord 后缺谓语，且后接双宾 him + that 从句，需及物动词过去式\n【依据】notify sb that… 「通知某人……」，房东通知涨租正是此场景\nHe was leaving our community because his landlord __(31)__ him that his rent was\ngoing to go up by $600 a month.——他要搬离，因为房东「通知」他月租要涨 600 美\n元。notify sb that 从句句型严丝合缝，因果链（被通知涨租 → 被迫搬走）完全通顺。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) anywhere", "B) asserts", "C) constituent", "D) correlated", "E) determining", "F) devastating", "G) enacted", "H) entails", "I) exemption", "J) impose", "K) notified", "L) notwithstanding", "M) presume", "N) rehabilitation", "O) reproaching"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) asserts\n动词\n【词性槽】单数主语 The association 后缺谓语，需三单动词接 that 从句\n【依据】assert that… 「坚称」，正是机构表态反对的措辞\nThe Rental Housing Association of Washington opposes the legislation. The\nassociation __(32)__ that the policy won't actually solve the problem of rental prices.\n——协会反对立法，「坚称」该政策解决不了租金问题。assert 接 that 从句表强硬主张，\n与前句 opposes 的反对立场无缝衔接。\n【竞争词】H) entails entail 是及物动词接名词性成分，不接 that 从句\nentails 意为「使必要、涉及」，用法是 A entails B（B 为名词/动名词），如 The job entails\ntravel；它没有 entail that 从句表「声称」的用法，代入后句法不成立。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 H) —— entails 与 asserts 同为三单，形态零区分度，最易凭模糊\n词义选错。The association __(32)__ that the policy won't actually solve the problem\nof rental prices. 中 entails 词性、时态全部合格，「涉及问题」的字面意思又和语境沾边，\n是最强干扰。防坑口诀：空后是 that 从句时先问动词能不能带宾语从句——entail 只能接\n名词性宾语，assert 才能接 that 从句表「坚称」，句法门槛一过见分晓。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) anywhere", "B) asserts", "C) constituent", "D) correlated", "E) determining", "F) devastating", "G) enacted", "H) entails", "I) exemption", "J) impose", "K) notified", "L) notwithstanding", "M) presume", "N) rehabilitation", "O) reproaching"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) enacted\n动词\n【词性槽】has been 之后需过去分词，构成现在完成被动语态\n【依据】法律政策「被颁布实施」用 enact，法案语域的专属动词\nThe rent control policy has failed everywhere it has been __(33)__ in this county.——\n租金管控政策「凡是在本县被实施过的地方都失败了」。enact 专用于「颁布法律、实施\n政策」，与 policy 构成最自然的被动搭配，也是说话者（反对派）论证政策无效的语义\n所需。\n【竞争词】K) notified notify 的对象是人，政策不能被「通知」\nnotified 意为「被通知」，其逻辑主语必须是人（sb be notified of/that…）；it 指 policy\n（物），「政策被通知」语义不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) anywhere", "B) asserts", "C) constituent", "D) correlated", "E) determining", "F) devastating", "G) enacted", "H) entails", "I) exemption", "J) impose", "K) notified", "L) notwithstanding", "M) presume", "N) rehabilitation", "O) reproaching"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) anywhere\n副词\n【词性槽】句子主干完整（It has not fixed one housing problem），空在句尾作状语，需副词\n【依据】否定句 + anywhere = 「在任何地方都不」，承接上句 everywhere\nThe rent control policy has failed everywhere it has been __(33)__ in this county. It\nhas not fixed one housing problem __(34)__.——上句说政策「处处失败」，本句递进\n「它在任何地方都没能解决哪怕一个住房问题」。not…anywhere 是英语否定的标准搭配，\n与 everywhere 形成语义对仗，语气一气呵成。\n【竞争词】L) notwithstanding notwithstanding 表让步「尽管如此」，与本句递进逻辑冲突\nnotwithstanding 作副词意为「尽管如此」（表让步转折），而本句与上句是递进强调（处处失\n败 → 一个问题都没解决），不是让步；且 notwithstanding 通常不单独置于句尾修饰否定陈述\n句，代入后逻辑和句法双不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) anywhere", "B) asserts", "C) constituent", "D) correlated", "E) determining", "F) devastating", "G) enacted", "H) entails", "I) exemption", "J) impose", "K) notified", "L) notwithstanding", "M) presume", "N) rehabilitation", "O) reproaching"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) determining\n动词\n【词性槽】when 引导的省略状语从句 when (it is) doing，需现在分词\n【依据】determine who gets the benefits「决定谁受益」，与 distinguish 的批评逻辑一致\nIt also does not distinguish between rich and poor when __(35)__ who gets the\nbenefits of rent control——批评者说该政策「在决定谁享受租金管控的好处时不区分贫\n富」。determine + 宾语从句（决定……）搭配自然，且与前文 it will punish small\nlandlords and allow wealthy renters easy access to cheap housing 的批评（富人也能\n占便宜）完全同向。\n【竞争词】O) reproaching reproach 意为「责备」，其对象是人且与 who gets… 从句不搭\nreproach 的用法是 reproach sb (for sth)，宾语是被责备的人；「在责备谁获得好处时不区分\n贫富」语义拧巴——政策不会去「责备」受益人，批评的对象错位，逻辑不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Pieter Levels created a chat system to satisfy the need of digital nomads for information tourists normally don't seek.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Pieter Levels / chat system / tourists 不问的信息\n【定位】Somewhere in his nomadic journey, Levels noticed that there was a growing need to create an\nonline infrastructure for people like himself. That's when he created #nomads, a membership-\nbased chat system which runs on the team communication platform known as Slack. The\nchannel allows users to ask people in their current city the types of practical questions that a\ntourist would unlikely need to ask.\n在他游牧旅途的某个时刻，Levels 注意到有必要为像他一样的人搭建一套线上基础设施。于\n是他创建了 #nomads，一个基于会员制的聊天系统，运行在名为 Slack 的团队通讯平台\n上。该频道允许用户向所在城市的人询问一些游客不太需要问的实用问题。\n【改写】Pieter Levels created a chat system ↔\nLevels... created #nomads, a membership-based chat system which runs on the team\ncommunication platform known as Slack\n同义替换 + 具体化\nto satisfy the need of digital nomads for information tourists normally don't seek ↔\nthere was a growing need to create an online infrastructure for people like himself... ask\npeople... the types of practical questions that a tourist would unlikely need to ask\n需求 + 信息需求具体化\n【辨邻】 C 该段也提到Pieter Levels，看似与题干人物相关，但全段只讲他如何选择工作地点，完\n全没有出现聊天系统或信息需求。\nPieter Levels... chooses his location at will, based on a variety of factors—Wi-Fi speed, average\ntemperature, exotic food and cost of living\nE 该段解释了digital nomads是什么，也提到群体数量增长，但没有提到 Pieter Levels\n创建聊天系统以满足信息需求。\nMembers of the geographically dispersed subculture that Levels is a part of call themselves\n\"digital nomads\"", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "It is said that digital nomads do not enjoy as much freedom as most people suppose.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】freedom / most people suppose\n【定位】When you're a digital nomad, you don't actually have as much freedom as most people think\nyou do. We have deadlines, we all have people relying on us and a mouth (or two) that needs\nto be fed.\n当你是数字游民时，你其实不像大多数人想的那样自由。我们有截止日期，有人依靠我们，\n还有一张（或两张）嘴要喂。\n【改写】digital nomads do not enjoy as much freedom as most people suppose ↔\nyou don't actually have as much freedom as most people think you do\n同义替换 + 句式微调\nIt is said that ↔ Adams said... it's completely true\n观点归属\n【辨邻】 A 该段提到roaming the globe from café to café is not without its challenges，看似\n讲困难，但并未说'自由不如人们想象'，挑战与自由度是两个概念。\nBut roaming the globe from café to café is not without its challenges\nH 该段讲nomad life... has its downsides，包括 Wi-Fi 和住宿选择，但核心不是'人们误\n以为很自由'，与题干语义不同。\nnomad life in foreign cities has its downsides", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Different from tourists, digital nomads travel to foreign places with work to do.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】Different from tourists / work to do\n【定位】But unlike a tourist, they don't arrive in a foreign city without any responsibilities. Travel isn't\nnecessarily required for them to do their jobs, but neither is staying in one place.\n但与游客不同，他们并非毫无责任地来到一座外国城市。旅行对他们的工作来说未必必要，\n但呆在一个地方也未必必要。\n【改写】Different from tourists, digital nomads travel to foreign places with work to do ↔\nunlike a tourist, they don't arrive in a foreign city without any responsibilities... do their jobs\n双重否定转肯定 + 概括\ndigital nomads travel to foreign places ↔\nthey generally enter a country on a tourist visa... arrive in a foreign city\n同义改写\n【辨邻】 G 该段提到It's completely different from vacationing，似乎与'不同于游客'有关，但\n强调的是沉浸城市生活、由成本驱动，未涉及'工作责任'。\nIt's completely different from vacationing—you really sink into the city life and get your feet wet in\nthe communities\nE 该段说数字游民与城市的互动方式entirely different from a tourist，但讲的是\nborderless/self-employed，未具体说明到达目的地时带有工作责任。\nThey are borderless, mostly self-employed remote workers who interact with cities in a way that's\nentirely different from a tourist", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Digital nomads believe their lifestyle enables them to broaden their vision and do a better job.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】broaden their vision / do a better job\n【定位】But nomads like Adams say that while they are aware of this privilege, they believe living\nthis way elevates what they're able to offer, by giving them a broader perspective on what\nthey're building, writing, or designing and expanding their world view.\n但像 Adams 这样的游民说，虽然他们意识到这种特权，但他们相信这种生活方式提升了他\n们能提供的价值，因为它让他们对自己正在构建、书写或设计的事物有了更广阔的视角，并\n扩展了他们的世界观。\n【改写】their lifestyle enables them to broaden their vision ↔\nliving this way... giving them a broader perspective... and expanding their world view\n同义短语替换\ndo a better job ↔ elevates what they're able to offer\n概括上位\n【辨邻】 L 该段说旅行让人stay inspired、成为更 sensible 的人，但核心是'保持灵感、欣赏他人\n创意'，未说因此把工作做得更好。\nBeing able to travel and get around makes it easy to stay inspired\nM 该段讲global awareness改变了 Levels 的公民观念和交友圈，但并未把这种意识与'把\n工作做得更好'联系起来。\nThis global awareness has been the reason I started Nomad List", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "The first thing a typical traveller possibly does after arriving at a city is search for information online about its popular tourist attractions.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】typical traveller / first thing / popular tourist attractions\n【定位】When the average traveller lands in a city like Bangkok, Berlin, or Buenos Aires, their first\nstep might be to drop their bags at their hotel and to log on to TripAdvisor to find out the\nhours of a popular tourist attraction or the best rooftop bar for happy hour.\n当普通旅行者抵达曼谷、柏林或布宜诺斯艾利斯这类城市时，他们的第一步可能是把行李放\n到酒店，然后登录 TripAdvisor 查询热门旅游景点的开放时间，或 happy hour 最佳屋顶\n酒吧。\n【改写】The first thing a typical traveller possibly does after arriving at a city ↔\nWhen the average traveller lands in a city... their first step might be to drop their bags at their\nhotel and to log on to TripAdvisor\n同义替换 + 具体化\nsearch for information online about its popular tourist attractions ↔\nlog on to TripAdvisor to find out the hours of a popular tourist attraction\n具体平台替换概括说法\n【辨邻】 C 该段开头But people like Pieter Levels are not your average traveller包含'average\ntraveller'这一锚点词，但讲的是数字游民不是普通旅行者，与题干所述普通旅行者行\n为无关。\nBut people like Pieter Levels are not your average traveller", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "One digital nomad thinks their life in foreign cities has its disadvantages.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】disadvantages / foreign cities\n【定位】Though Adams frequently posts pictures on Instagram of his enviable cooking adventures\nfrom Seoul to Bangkok, he says nomad life in foreign cities has its downsides, including\nfinding adequate Wi-Fi for the bandwidth-hungry work his livelihood depends on.\n尽管 Adams 经常在 Instagram 上晒从首尔到曼谷令人羡慕的烹饪冒险照片，但他说外国城\n市里的游牧生活也有弊端，包括为他赖以生计的高带宽工作寻找足够的 Wi-Fi。\n【改写】One digital nomad thinks their life in foreign cities has its disadvantages ↔\nhe says nomad life in foreign cities has its downsides\n同义词替换\ntheir life in foreign cities ↔ nomad life in foreign cities\n原样复现\n【辨邻】 A 该段提到roaming the globe from café to café is not without its challenges，但这\n是对数字游民整体的泛述，没有具体讲'外国城市生活'的 downside。\nBut roaming the globe from café to café is not without its challenges\nI 该段说数字游民生活not without its challenges，也提到 deadline、责任，但重点不\n是'外国城市生活'的弊端，而是自由与责任的反差。\nIt's nice to be able to work anywhere in the world but it's not without its challenges", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Travelling around helps to keep digital nomads inspired.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Travelling around / inspired\n【定位】\"Being able to travel and get around makes it easy to stay inspired,\" he added. \"I truly believe\nthat it makes me a more sensible human being, as I tend to see and appreciate the creative\nsolutions people come up with in other parts of the world, even though many tend to have far\nmore constraints than we have at home.\"\n\"能够旅行和四处走动让人容易保持灵感，\"他补充道。\"我真的相信这使我成为一个更明事理\n的人，因为我倾向于看到并欣赏世界其他地方的人们想出的创造性解决方案，尽管他们中的\n许多人往往比我们在国内面临更多限制。\"\n【改写】Travelling around helps to keep digital nomads inspired ↔\nBeing able to travel and get around makes it easy to stay inspired\n同义改写 + 具体化\n【辨邻】 K 该段讲旅行带来broader perspective和expanding their world view，是工作产出层\n面，与题干'保持灵感'不同。\nby giving them a broader perspective on what they're building, writing, or designing and expanding\ntheir world view\nM 该段讲旅行带来global awareness并改变交友圈，但未提到'保持灵感'。\nThis global awareness has been the reason I started Nomad List", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "The number of self-employed remote workers living a digital nomad's life is fast increasing.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】self-employed remote workers / fast increasing\n【定位】A relatively new phenomenon, there are no hard statistics on the prevalence of digital nomads\nor location independent workers. But thanks to the growing prevalence of freelance\nlivelihoods, a global sharing economy and numerous digital tools, the number of people who\nare able to live this life is growing quickly.\n作为一种相对较新的现象，目前关于数字游民或地点独立工作者流行程度的硬数据还很少。\n但由于自由职业生活方式日益普遍、全球共享经济以及众多数字工具的出现，能够过上这种\n生活的人数正在快速增长。\n【改写】The number of self-employed remote workers living a digital nomad's life is fast increasing ↔\nthe number of people who are able to live this life is growing quickly\n同义替换 + 概括\nself-employed remote workers ↔ mostly self-employed remote workers\n原样复现\n【辨邻】 A 该段提到the growing prevalence of remote-based work，看似与题干相关，但这\n是背景，未说数字游民人数快速增长。\nthanks to the growing prevalence of remote-based work\nF 该段继续描述数字游民与游客/居民的区别，提到they don't work for a large\nmultinational company，但未涉及人数增长。\nUnlike a resident expat, they don't work for a large multinational company in an air conditioned\noffice building", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Pieter Levels says his life as a nomad has transformed not only his idea of citizenship, but his choice of companions.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Pieter Levels / citizenship / choice of companions\n【定位】Levels says that the nomad life has not only changed his notions of citizenship, but the people\nhe chooses to spend time with as well. \"Saying you're a 'citizen of the world' is a super cliché,\nright? But it's becoming quite true,\" Levels said.\nLevels 说，游牧生活不仅改变了他的公民观念，也改变了他选择共度时光的人。\"说自己\n是'世界公民'非常老套，对吧？但这正变得越来越真实，\"Levels 说。\n【改写】his life as a nomad has transformed not only his idea of citizenship ↔\nthe nomad life has not only changed his notions of citizenship\n同义替换\nbut his choice of companions ↔ but the people he chooses to spend time with as well\n概括上位\n【辨邻】 A 该段提到\"citizens of the world\"这一说法，但未说 Levels 的公民观念因此改变。\nFor those \"citizens of the world\", the office can be anything from a beach hut in Brisbane to a\nStarbucks in Seattle\nE 该段说数字游民borderless，看似与'世界公民'相关，但未涉及 Levels 个人对交友选择\n的变化。\nThey are borderless, mostly self-employed remote workers", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Interactions with local people and institutions provide nomads with far more cultural information than a visit to the national museum.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】local people and institutions / national museum / cultural information\n【定位】But these challenges can often provide an opportunity for nomads to see a side of a city they\nmay not have if they were staying in a luxury hotel for a long weekend. However\ninconvenient, a trip to a local doctor, immigration office or laundry—or a conflict with a local\nAirbnb host (the accommodation style of choice for most nomads)—can be far more\nculturally instructive than a trip to the national museum.\n但这些挑战往往能给游民提供机会，让他们看到如果只是在豪华酒店度长周末所看不到的城\n市一面。无论多么不便，去看当地医生、移民局或洗衣店，或与当地 Airbnb 房东发生冲突\n——这些都是大多数游民首选的住宿方式——都可能比去国家博物馆更有文化教益。\n【改写】Interactions with local people and institutions provide nomads with far more cultural information\nthan a visit to the national museum\n↔\na trip to a local doctor, immigration office or laundry—or a conflict with a local Airbnb host...\ncan be far more culturally instructive than a trip to the national museum\n概括上位 + 比较结构\nInteractions with local people and institutions ↔\na trip to a local doctor, immigration office or laundry—or a conflict with a local Airbnb host\n具体化 ↔ 概括\n【辨邻】 G 该段说sink into the city life and get your feet wet in the communities，看似与本\n地互动有关，但未与'国家博物馆'作比较，也未说这些互动'更有文化教益'。\nyou really sink into the city life and get your feet wet in the communities\nI 该段提到数字游民生活not without its challenges，但讲的是 deadlines 和责任，不\n是挑战带来的文化学习机会。\nit's nice to be able to work anywhere in the world but it's not without its challenges", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What does the author believe regarding social media?", "options": ["A) To stop children using it is unrealistic and inadvisable.", "B) It helps both adults and children to organise their lives.", "C) It poses problems to both parents and children.", "D) A world without it is really hard to imagine."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author believe regarding social media? 问的是作者对一个明确话\n题的具体判断，有确定对象可回文查找，属于观点类细节题。\n【拆句】Q46 是 What 引导的简单疑问句，regarding social media 是介词短语限定话题范围，无\n否定、无比较、无长修饰，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】While younger children should not be on platforms where 13 is the minimum age, it is neither\npractical nor desirable to imagine a world in which teenagers are prevented from accessing\nthe platforms and messaging systems that the adults around them use to organise their lives.\n虽然更年幼的儿童不该使用最低年龄为13岁的平台，但想象一个青少年被禁止使用周遭成年\n人用来安排生活的平台和通讯系统的世界，既不现实也不可取。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nneither practical nor desirable ↔ unrealistic and inadvisable\nteenagers are prevented from accessing the platforms ↔ To stop children using it\n预判'禁止孩子使用不现实也不明智'与 To stop children using it is unrealistic and\ninadvisable. 的行为、评价两层完全对应，锁定 A。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文只说平台是 the adults around them use to organise their lives——用来安排生\n活的是成年人，并未说社交媒体帮助儿童安排生活；It helps both adults and children\nto organise their lives. 把用途主体扩大成'成年人和儿童双方'。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文是 Children's use of social media is a problem.——'儿童使用社交媒体是个问\n题'，问题的承载者是儿童的使用行为；It poses problems to both parents and\nchildren. 改写成'给父母和孩子双方都带来问题'，parents 一方原文从未提及。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文 it is neither practical nor desirable to imagine a world 是评价'这种世界不现实\n也不可取'，不是说这个世界难以想象；A world without it is really hard to imagine.\n把价值判断偷换成认知难度判断，且暗含'没有它的世界无法想象'，与作者'年幼儿童本就\n不该用'的前提相悖。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about British teenage girls?", "options": ["A) The more frequently they interact on social media, the more they experience harassment.", "B) The more time they spend on social media, the more likely they suffer from depression.", "C) Most of them are engaged on social media for at least three hours a day.", "D) They spend half as many hours on social media as British teenage boys."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the passage about British teenage girls? 是典型的文中事实提\n问，要求找出关于该群体的哪个说法与原文相符。\n【拆句】Q47 主干 What do we learn，from the passage 与 about British teenage girls 两个介\n词短语分别限定来源与对象，无语法陷阱；注意四个备选都是 the more...the more 或数量\n比较的说法，真正的难点在选项侧的比较关系。\n【定位】The finding that the alarmingly high rate of depression in British teenage girls is closely\ncorrelated to time spent on social media is extremely concerning.\n英国少女抑郁症比例高得惊人、且与花在社交媒体上的时间密切相关，这一发现极其令人\n担忧。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\n原文只说 More than a third of depressed girls have experienced online\nharassment.——抑郁女孩中超过三分之一遭遇过网络骚扰，这是一个静态比例；The\nmore frequently they interact on social media, the more they experience\nharassment. 把它升级成'互动越频繁骚扰越多'的递增关系，原文没有任何依据。\nB) ✓ 正确\nis closely correlated to time spent on social media ↔\nThe more time they spend on social media, the more likely they suffer from depression\nthe alarmingly high rate of depression in British teenage girls ↔ they suffer from depression\n预判'时间越多越可能抑郁'与 The more time they spend on social media, the more\nlikely they suffer from depression. 完全同向同义；其余三项都在数据的主语或比例上\n动了手脚，锁定 B。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文是 two in five girls are on social media for at least three hours a day——五分\n之二（40%）是少数；Most of them are engaged on social media for at least\nthree hours a day. 用 Most（大多数，>50%）替换，数量范围被明显夸大。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文 with half as many boys engaged to the same extent 说的是：达到同等使用\n强度的男生人数是女生的一半；They spend half as many hours on social media as\nBritish teenage boys. 反说成'女生花的时间是男生的一半'，比较的主体与方向全部\n颠倒。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What is the author's view on the research finding about British teenage girls?", "options": ["A) Its implications about online abuse are obvious.", "B) Its key message calls for immediate attention.", "C) Its importance should not be undervalued.", "D) Its uncertainty should not go unnoticed."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What is the author's view on the research finding about British teenage girls? 问的是\n对一个明确对象（研究发现）的看法，该对象在文中有确定位置，属于观点类细节题而非全\n文态度题。\n【拆句】Q48 主干 What is the author's view，on the research finding 限定评价对象，about\nBritish teenage girls 进一步限定是哪一项发现。两层介词短语嵌套，读题时注意评价对象\n是 finding 而不是 girls 本身。\n【定位】Although the research does not prove that social media causes depression, the importance of\nthe finding that there is a connection should not be underestimated.\n尽管这项研究并未证明社交媒体导致抑郁，但'两者存在关联'这一发现的重要性不应被\n低估。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文说 Schools have done important work in communicating key messages\nabout the risks of online abuse.——传达网络虐待风险是学校的已有工作；Its\nimplications about online abuse are obvious. 声称该发现对网络虐待的'启示显而易\n见'，obvious 在原文无任何对应，且与后文 many professionals as well as parents\nare confused and uncertain 的'困惑不确定'相抵触。\nB) 过度推断\n作者的评价止步于'重要性不应被低估'；Its key message calls for immediate\nattention. 进一步主张'需要立即关注/行动'，immediate 这层紧迫性是选项自行加码\n——第二段的 is extremely concerning 虽令人担忧，但也不等于'要求立即处置'。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe importance of the finding that there is a connection should not be underestimated ↔\nIts importance should not be undervalued.\n预判'重要性不应被低估'与 Its importance should not be undervalued. 仅一词之差\n（underestimated→undervalued），是本卷最直白的一组同义替换，锁定 C。\nD) 与原文相反\nIts uncertainty should not go unnoticed. 揪住'不确定性'不放，但这正是 Although\n从句 the research does not prove that social media causes depression 里作者主动\n让步、并用主句压过去的内容；作者强调的是重要性，不是提醒人们注意不确定性，选\n项与作者着力的方向相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What do experts think of health secretary Matt Hancock's idea?", "options": ["A) Its implementation requires prompt action.", "B) It is a welcome initiative but is long overdue.", "C) Its success depends on how it is implemented.", "D) It is too simplistic to produce the desired effect."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What do experts think of health secretary Matt Hancock's idea? 主体（experts）与对\n象（Hancock's idea）都明确可定位，是观点类细节题；只是正确项需要把原文两处评价整\n合后认出，比对要求更高。\n【拆句】Q49 主干 What do experts think，of health secretary Matt Hancock's idea 限定对\n象；health secretary 是 Matt Hancock 的同位语官衔，'s idea 表明考的是专家对其提议\n的评价，不是对 Hancock 本人的评价。\n【定位】This idea has the virtue of simplicity, though some experts question its effects and call for a\ngreater degree of differentiation, arguing that time online by itself is not a bad thing and it all\ndepends on what you are doing.\n这个想法有简单这一优点，不过一些专家质疑其效果，并主张更大程度的区别对待，理由是\n上网时间本身并非坏事，关键在于你在做什么。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nIts implementation requires prompt action. 称实施需要迅速行动，但专家对效果的\n质疑与'快慢'无关，prompt action 在原文没有任何对应表述，属于凭空添加的主张。\nB) 张冠李戴\nIt is a welcome initiative but is long overdue. 里，welcome 出自作者对两项举措的\n总评 Both initiatives are welcome.，long overdue 出自作者对监管巨头滞后的批评\nStatutory regulation of the digital media giants is long overdue.——两句都是作者\n的声音，且 overdue 说的还不是这个想法本身；题干问的是专家。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文专家说 it all depends on what you are doing——效果取决于你在网上做什么\n（内容），Its success depends on how it is implemented. 偷换成'取决于如何实\n施'；depends on 句型原样照搬，宾语却被整个换掉。\nD) ✓ 正确\nhas the virtue of simplicity ↔ too simplistic\nsome experts question its effects ↔ to produce the desired effect\n预判'太简单、难奏效'正是 It is too simplistic to produce the desired effect. 的字面\n意思，且每一半都能在定位句找到出处（simplicity / question its effects），锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "Besides social media companies, what factors contribute to young people's psychological health problems?", "options": ["A) Poorly financed schools and health services.", "B) Politicians' eagerness to divert public attention.", "C) Bad statutory regulation of digital media giants.", "D) Their parents' educational background and life."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】Besides social media companies, what factors contribute to young people's\npsychological health problems? 问文中列举了哪些其他成因，有明确的列举句可查，属\n细节题。\n【拆句】Q50 主干 what factors contribute to young people's psychological health\nproblems，句首 Besides social media companies 是介词短语作排除限定。contribute\nto 表示'促成'，问的是成因；Besides 是全题题眼，决定答案不能落在社交媒体公司头上。\n【定位】Of course, social media companies are not the source of all our ills, and can offer a too-\neasy target for politicians keen to divert attention from inadequately resourced schools and\nhealth services.\n当然，社交媒体公司并非我们所有问题的根源，而且它们很容易成为政客们的靶子——政客\n热衷于借此转移人们对投入不足的学校和医疗服务的关注。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ninadequately resourced schools and health services ↔\nPoorly financed schools and health services.\n预判候选'投入不足的学校与医疗服务'与 Poorly financed schools and health\nservices. 仅修饰语有别，主体名词逐字相同，且它确属'社交媒体公司以外'的因素，锁\n定 A。\nB) 答非所问\nPoliticians' eagerness to divert public attention. 取自 politicians keen to divert\nattention，但政客转移视线是对问题的掩盖手段，不是导致年轻人心理问题的成因；题\n干问的是 contribute to 心理问题的因素，此项答非所问。\nC) 答非所问\nBad statutory regulation of digital media giants. 借自 P4 Statutory regulation of\nthe digital media giants is long overdue.——监管缺位针对的正是数字媒体巨头本\n身，属于'社交媒体公司'这一条线，而题干用 Besides social media companies 明确把\n这条线排除在外；且 P5 列举的其他原因里也根本没有它。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文的另一成因是 increasing pressure to succeed in education and in life more\nbroadly——年轻人自己在教育和人生上的成功压力；Their parents' educational\nbackground and life. 把 education and life 的原词留下，却硬是安上 parents' 这个原\n文没有的主体，把'自身压力'偷换成'父母背景'。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What do we learn about the G-20's agreement on curbing the use of tax havens?", "options": ["A) It has aggravated the problem in non-G-20 countries.", "B) It has taken a toll on countries such as Cayman Islands.", "C) It has failed to achieve the expected outcome.", "D) It has failed to result in any long-term plan."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about the G-20's agreement...? 询问关于该协议的事实信息，没有\ninfer/suggest/imply 等推断标志。\n【拆句】What do we learn about the G-20's agreement on curbing the use of tax havens? 为\n主谓宾结构，介词 on 后接动名词短语作后置修饰，整体无否定、比较等陷阱，平铺直叙。\n【定位】About a decade ago, the G-20, a forum of the world's biggest economies, agreed to crack\ndown on multinational corporations' abusive use of tax havens. This resulted in a plan that\naimed to curb practices that shielded a large chunk of corporate profits from tax authorities.\nBut, instead of reining in the use of tax havens—countries such as the Bahamas and Cayman\nIslands with very low or no effective tax rates—the problem has only gotten worse.\n大约十年前，世界最大经济体论坛 G-20 同意打击跨国企业滥用避税天堂的行为。这产生了\n一项计划，旨在遏制将大量企业利润 shield 在税务机关之外的做法。但是，非但没有遏制\n避税天堂的使用——例如巴哈马和开曼群岛等实际税率极低或为零的国家——问题反而变得\n更糟。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文只说 the problem has only gotten worse，未限定问题恶化的地理范围；选项凭\n空添加 non-G-20 countries。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文 countries such as the Bahamas and Cayman Islands 是 tax havens 的举例，选\n项却把它们说成协议的受害者。\nC) ✓ 正确\nagreed to crack down on multinational corporations' abusive use of tax havens. This resulted in a\nplan that aimed to curb practices\n↔ the expected outcome\nthe problem has only gotten worse ↔ failed to achieve\nIt has failed to achieve the expected outcome. 准确概括了 计划旨在遏制 与 问题反\n而恶化 之间的转折关系。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文 This resulted in a plan 明确说明协议产生了计划，选项却说 failed to result in\nany long-term plan。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What mainly accounted for the large increase in the world's biggest multinational corporations' shift of profits to tax havens?", "options": ["A) Fluctuating tax rates globally.", "B) Lenient policies of the U.S.", "C) Heavy taxes imposed on them.", "D) Rapid growth of their business."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What mainly accounted for...? 直接询问导致某现象的主要原因，属于因果型细节题。\n【拆句】What mainly accounted for the large increase in the world's biggest multinational\ncorporations' shift of profits to tax havens? 中，mainly accounted for 是因果谓语，\nin... 是 the large increase 的后置定语，整体问的是利润转移大幅增长的主因。\n【定位】The main reasons for the large increase were the growth of the tax avoidance industry in\nthe 1980s and U.S. policies that made it easier to shift profits from high-tax countries to\ntax havens.\n大幅增长的主要原因是 1980 年代避税产业的兴起，以及美国政策使得将利润从高税率国家\n转移到避税天堂变得更容易。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文只提到全球有效企业税率从 23% 降至 17%，这是单向下降而非 fluctuating（波\n动），更非利润转移激增的主因。\nB) ✓ 正确\nU.S. policies that made it easier to shift profits from high-tax countries to tax havens ↔\nLenient policies of the U.S.\nshift profits from high-tax countries to tax havens ↔ shift of profits to tax havens\nLenient policies of the U.S. 是 U.S. policies that made it easier to shift profits... 的\n同义概括。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文 high-tax countries 指税率较高的国家，选项却偷换为 Heavy taxes imposed on\nthem（对企业征收重税），将国家属性错误地套到企业上。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文 growth 的主语是 the tax avoidance industry，选项却改成 their business（跨\n国企业业务），主体被替换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What have policymakers been doing to prevent government tax loss?", "options": ["A) Reducing corporate taxes step by step.", "B) Scrapping consumption taxes gradually.", "C) Making joint efforts to levy corporate taxes.", "D) Adjusting global effective corporate tax rates."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What have policymakers been doing...? 询问主体已采取的行动，属于事实型细节题。\n【拆句】What have policymakers been doing to prevent government tax loss? 为现在完成进\n行时，强调动作从过去持续到现在，to prevent... 表目的，句法较平实。\n【定位】So far, the world as a whole has been trying to solve this problem by cutting or scrapping\ncorporate taxes in a very gradual way.\n到目前为止，全世界作为一个整体，一直在试图通过以非常渐进的方式削减或取消企业所得\n税来解决这个问题。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ncutting or scrapping corporate taxes in a very gradual way ↔\nReducing corporate taxes step by step.\nReducing corporate taxes step by step. 是 cutting or scrapping corporate taxes in\na very gradual way 的简洁同义改写。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文 governments have relied more heavily on consumption taxes 说明政府更依赖\n消费税，而非取消消费税。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文说 cutting or scrapping corporate taxes（削减或取消企业所得税），选项却说\nmaking joint efforts to levy corporate taxes（联合征收企业所得税），方向相反且添\n加 joint efforts 这一未提及信息。\nD) 以偏概全\n原文 the global effective corporate tax rate has fallen from 23% to 17% 是削减企\n业所得税后的结果，选项却把它说成政策制定者'正在做的事'本身。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does the author say would bring tax havens to an end?", "options": ["A) The alignment of corporate taxes with consumption taxes.", "B) The implementation of generous tax rates in all countries.", "C) The agreement on the root cause of global profit-shifting.", "D) The imposition of a global minimum corporate tax rate."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say would bring tax havens to an end? 直接询问作者提出的条\n件与结果，没有 infer/suggest 等推断标志。\n【拆句】What does the author say would bring tax havens to an end? 中，What 引导宾语从\n句，would bring... to an end 是固定短语表'使...终结'，整体询问作者提出的充分条件。\n【定位】If countries could agree on a global minimum corporate tax rate of, say, 20%, the problem of\nprofit-shifting would largely disappear, as tax havens would simply cease to exist.\n如果各国能就比如说 20% 的全球最低企业所得税率达成一致，利润转移问题就会基本消\n失，因为避税天堂将不复存在。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文 P6 提到企业所得税与消费税并存，但从未说二者需要 alignment（协调一致）来\n终结避税天堂。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文 the root cause of profit-shifting is the incentives involved, such as generous\nor lenient corporate tax rates in other countries 指出慷慨/宽松税率是问题根源，而\n非解决方案。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文说要 agree on a global minimum corporate tax rate，选项偷换为 agree on the\nroot cause of global profit-shifting，对象完全不同。\nD) ✓ 正确\nagree on a global minimum corporate tax rate ↔\nThe imposition of a global minimum corporate tax rate.\ntax havens would simply cease to exist ↔ bring tax havens to an end\nThe imposition of a global minimum corporate tax rate. 对应 agree on a global\nminimum corporate tax rate，是使避税天堂消失的条件。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2026-06-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What could be expected of the global problem of profit-shifting in the near future?", "options": ["A) It would be solved with more countries cutting tax rates.", "B) It would end with governments' awareness of its severity.", "C) It would cease to exist with worldwide corporate support.", "D) It would continue because of noncooperation from the U.S."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （推断题）\n【判型】What could be expected of... in the near future? 要求根据文中当前事实推断未来趋势，\n含有 could / expected / near future 等推断与未来指向词。\n【拆句】What could be expected of the global problem of profit-shifting in the near future?\n中，could be expected of 表示'对...可以预期什么'，in the near future 限定时间范围，整\n体为推断语气。\n【定位】This type of mechanism is exactly what more than 130 countries signed onto in 2021, with\nimplementation of a 15% minimum tax set to begin soon in the EU, U.K., Japan, Indonesia\nand many other countries. The U.S. has notably not been able to pass this legislation. Our\nresearch suggests implementing this type of tax reform is necessary in reversing the shift of\never-greater amounts of corporate profits to tax havens—instead of being taxed by the\ngovernments where they operate and create value.\n这种机制正是 130 多个国家在 2021 年签署的，15% 最低税的实施即将在欧盟、英国、日\n本、印度尼西亚和许多其他国家开始。但美国显然未能通过这项立法。我们的研究表明，实\n施这种税收改革对于扭转企业利润越来越多地流向避税天堂的趋势是必要的——这些利润本\n应由其运营和创造价值所在地的政府征税。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文 P6-P7 指出削减企业所得税是过去做法且未解决问题，作者主张的是全球最低税率\n而非继续减税。\nB) 无中生有\n原文未提及 governments' awareness of its severity（各国政府对其严重性的认识）会\n终结该问题。\nC) 无中生有\n原文讨论的是各国政府的政策行动，未提及 worldwide corporate support（全球企业\n支持）会终结利润转移。\nD) ✓ 正确\nThe U.S. has notably not been able to pass this legislation. ↔\nbecause of noncooperation from the U.S.\nimplementing this type of tax reform is necessary in reversing the shift of ever-greater amounts\nof corporate profits to tax havens\n↔ It would continue\nIt would continue because of noncooperation from the U.S. 从 美国未通过立法 与\n改革对扭转趋势必要 推得，未跨步。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}]});
